\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Republicans are a mess right now, and voters know it. Does the party?\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par 14:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The GOP is internally divided, filled with self-serving carnival-barkers, and beholden to cruel positions that appall most voters\par \par \uc2 It was supposed to be the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s last stand, in a state where they had exercised near-absolute control for years, and they had every hope of putting up a good fight. Yet in the end, it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t even close.\par \par \uc2 Millions of dollars had poured into Wisconsin ahead of an election for a seat on the state supreme court, a hotly contested race between a liberal judge and one backed by moneyed rightwing interests. The stakes were high: Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s state legislature has been gerrymandered out of competitiveness for more than a decade, for one thing, meaning that the state, which has a roughly even split between Democratic and Republican voters, had nevertheless become a grim experiment in one-party rule, with Republicans commanding a majority of statehouse seats despite receiving far fewer votes proportionally. Federal elections in Wisconsin seemed worryingly vulnerable, too: when Trump sued over his 2020 loss there, making spurious claims of election fraud, the Republican-controlled state supreme court ruled against him by only one vote.\par \par \uc2 But most pressing was \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision, the state was thrown into chaos when a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/03/21/politics/wisconsin-supreme-court-debate-abortion-1849-law/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wildly sexist law from 1849}}}\uc2 , banning all abortions, went back into effect. The law is poised to be challenged at the state supreme court, and the rights of half of the Wisconsin population could depend on who controls that court.\par \par \uc2 This is the face that Republicans are putting forward to the country as they head into the 2024 cycle: cruel, creepy, and corrupt.\par \par \uc2 One of the candidates in the judicial race, Janet Protasiewicz, was open about her beliefs: she frankly described herself as pro-choice. The other, Dan Kelly, brushed off questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But he was backed by anti-choice groups, and had long been on the payroll of the Republican party, including in representing them in legal matters pertaining to their 2020 election denial.\par \par \uc2 While Protasiewicz relied on small-dollar donations, Kelly raised plenty of money \u8211\'20\'13 much of it from large, out-of-state megadonor funds like the \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2 -backed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/04/04/1167815077/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results-abortion-voting-protasiewicz-kelly"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fair Courts America}}}\uc2 . But Protasiewicz blew him out of the park: she won with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/04/us/elections/results-wisconsin-supreme-court.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lead of 10 percentage points}}}\uc2 . Voters\u8217\'20\'19 top concern? \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s blowout victory in Wisconsin came on the same day that another high-stakes political contest was playing out in New York: the arraignment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on charges of falsifying business records. True to form, Trump incited a chaotic, carnivalesque, and extremely tacky scene outside the Manhattan criminal courthouse as the charges were read against him. The media breathlessly followed every moment of the arraignment, with cable-news cameras even following his plane from Mar-a-Lago, his gaudy resort home in Palm Beach, up to New York.\par \par \uc2 The arraignment has renewed attention on Trump, elevating him from the sideshow status that he has occupied for much of the Biden presidency back into the center of national attention. It has also made it seem much more likely \u8211\'20\'13 if still not inevitable \u8211\'20\'13 that he may become the Republican nominee. The party\u8217\'20\'19s elite wants to ditch him, and his rivals for the nomination want to defeat him, but Trump still reigns in the hearts of Republican voters. He\u8217\'20\'19s the one they\u8217\'20\'19re comparing every other candidate to \u8211\'20\'13 and why settle for imitations when you can have the real thing?\par \par \uc2 But Trump, still, represents a liability for the Republican party, even as he fulfills many of their voters\u8217\'20\'19 most ardent desires and least honorable impulses. He\u8217\'20\'19s a crooked buffoon, and he\u8217\'20\'19s getting weirder and worse; outside of the Republican party itself, most voters don\u8217\'20\'19t like him, don\u8217\'20\'19t find him funny any more, don\u8217\'20\'19t want to put up with his chaos, and don\u8217\'20\'19t like what he\u8217\'20\'19s done to their country.\par \par \uc2 The Republican party, then, is like the dog that caught the car: it had its deepest desires fulfilled, in the form of the withdrawal of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Dobbs, and in the form of a candidate who embodies its grievances, in Trump. Republicans got what they wanted. Now, they risk being destroyed by it.\par \par \uc2 The Republican party today is messy, internally divided, filled with self-serving carnival-barkers like Trump and his descendants \u8211\'20\'13 George Santos, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 more interested in garnering publicity for themselves than in securing electoral or policy victories for their party, and most of all, beholden to positions, most notably on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that inflict untold suffering and are increasingly abhorrent to the average voter.\par \par \uc2 Republicans propose sadistic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that are unpopular with voters, then reject amendments that would allow doctors to save women\u8217\'20\'19s lives without fear of prosecution \u8211\'20\'13 making a self-righteous display of their own femicidal barbarity. They enact bans on transgender girls in sports and harness the full power of the state against trans student athletes \u8211\'20\'13 who number, in many states, fewer than a dozen.\par \par \uc2 Although party elites periodically propose alternatives to Trump \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  this week, Glenn Youngkin the next \u8211\'20\'13 they as yet have no viable presidential candidates who seem able to mount a real challenge to the former president, a man whose legal liabilities are only going to worsen over the coming year, and whom voters already soundly rejected in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But the party\u8217\'20\'19s problems go beyond Trump and the extremist anti-choice regime that his judicial nominees have ushered in. The party has yielded to extremists in its base, placing candidates for office that range from the out of touch to the intensely creepy. Mehmet Oz, who ran for Senate in Pennsylvania last year, has the affect of a snake-oil salesman; \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , the \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  lackey who ran in Arizona, comes off as sinister. George Santos, a congressman from New York, seems to have lied about absolutely everything, possibly including his own name.\par \par \uc2 These candidates are not accidents: they are who the Republican voters chose in their primaries, and who the Republican party has cultivated. This is the face that Republicans are putting forward to the country as they head into the 2024 cycle: cruel, creepy, and corrupt.\par \par \uc2 Like the voters themselves, Republican party insiders don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to have much interest in changing course. In January, just weeks after anger over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and disgust at the extremism of Republican candidates drove the party to a historically weak showing in the 2022 midterms, the \uc2 Republican National Committee{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/01/27/ronna-mcdaniel-wins-her-race-for-rnc-chair-00079980"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 re-elected Ronna McDaniel}}}\uc2 , a Trump loyalist, for a fourth term as chair. As voters become more and more alienated from the party, Republicans seem serenely confident in their own losing strategy \u8211\'20\'13 or, at least, unwilling to change it. Like the Wisconsin supreme court race, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  contest became heated, with multiple factions hurling recriminations. But just as the liberal Protasiewicz had a blowout victory in her election, McDaniel\u8217\'20\'19s victory in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair election was a landslide, too.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230410ej4a001bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
