\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Progressives tout string of wins across US as template for Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 Joan E Greve in Washington \par \uc2 1,396 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2023\par 10:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Victories in Chicago and St Louis, plus a key judicial race in Wisconsin, argue against tacking to center, leftwingers say\par \par \uc2 Progressives in the midwest had three reasons to celebrate on Tuesday. In Wisconsin, the liberal judge Janet Protasiewicz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delivered a resounding victory}}}\uc2  in the state supreme court race, flipping control of the court for the first time in 15 years. In Chicago, the progressive mayoral candidate Brandon Johnson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/chicago-mayoral-election-polls-brandon-johnson-paul-vallas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prevailed}}}\uc2  over \uc2 Paul Vallas\uc2 , a more conservative Democrat who ran on a tough-on-crime message. And in St Louis, progressives won a majority of seats on the board of aldermen, the lawmaking body for the city.\par \par \uc2 As they took their victory lap, progressives made clear that they viewed the wins as merely the beginning of a broader trend in America\u8217\'20\'19s elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a multicultural, multi-generational movement that has literally captured the imagination of not just the city of Chicago but the rest of the world,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnson said in his victory speech. \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s take this bold progressive movement around these United States of America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several lessons can be learned from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s results, progressive leaders say. They hope their victories send a message to Democratic party leaders about the enduring resonance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the popularity of progressives\u8217\'20\'19 message and the importance of long-term grassroots organizing. The wins also come at a vital moment for progressives, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/30/biden-2024-policies-climate-oil-campaign-pledges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have criticized}}}\uc2  Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s recent move toward the political center on issues such as energy and crime.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re building a project all across this country, and that project is ascendant,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maurice Mitchell, national director of the \uc2 Working Families party\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s both a culmination of years of organizing, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a validation of the popular appeal of that project.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8216\'20\'18a winning issue for Democrats\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights appeared to weigh heavily on the minds of Wisconsin voters as they went to the polls on Tuesday. Wisconsin has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban dating back to 1849 on the books, and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have argued that the policy should be enforced following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reversal of Roe v Wade last summer.\par \par \uc2 The question of enforcing the 1849 ban is expected to soon come before the state supreme court, and the policy seems likely to be thrown out following Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s win.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think that there\u8217\'20\'19s a very great chance now that we\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to get this ban off the books and restore access to folks in Wisconsin,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ryan Stitzlein, senior national political director of the reproductive rights group Naral. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is life-changing for folks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz made the end of Roe a central focus of her campaign, emphasizing her personal support for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and attacking her opponent, conservative Dan Kelly, over his past work for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Wisconsin Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think you can overstate the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this race. Judge Janet led with her support for reproductive freedom,\u8221\'20\'1D Stitzlein said.\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz defeated Kelly by 11 points. The result was extraordinary for the battleground state of Wisconsin, which Biden won by less than one point in 2020. Four years earlier, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carried the state with 47.2% of the vote compared with Hillary Clinton\u8217\'20\'19s 46.5%.\par \par \uc2 To Stitzlein, Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s decisive win should dispel any lingering questions over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access continues to resonate with voters nearly a year after Roe was overturned. Before the 2022 elections, some Democratic strategists {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/04/democrats-scotus-abortion-midterms-00029810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggested}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not sufficiently move the needle with midterm voters, but progressive activists firmly rejected that reasoning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI actually feel strongly that the longer these bans are in place, the more energy and more anger that\u8217\'20\'19s going to be out there because there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be more people that are affected by it,\u8221\'20\'1D Stitzlein said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a winning issue for Democrats, for folks that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access because support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is not partisan, as was demonstrated on Tuesday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Not an overnight coalition\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 For progressive organizers in Chicago and St Louis, the victories on Tuesday were years in the making.\par \par \uc2 When Johnson turned his attention to the mayoral race in Chicago, he drew crucial support from his longtime progressive allies. He received hefty assistance from the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2 , where he has worked for the past 10 years as the organization pushed for progressive reform. And when Johnson ran for the Cook county board of commissioners in 2018, he received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unitedworkingfamilies.org/news/united-working-families-announces-2018-candidate-slate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an endorsement}}}\uc2  from the United Working Families, an affiliate of the \uc2 Working Families party\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBrandon is not an overnight sensation; the coalition that he built is not an overnight coalition,\u8221\'20\'1D Mitchell said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis was a coalition that had been measured and patient and consistent over years, slowly aggregating the power to be able to seize the victory on Tuesday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A similar story unfolded in St Louis on Tuesday. The progressive mayor, \uc2 Tishaura Jones\uc2 , and the board of aldermen president, Megan Green, endorsed a slate of candidates who were able to flip three seats on the board. Green, who became board president last year, served as a convention delegate for Bernie Sanders during his 2016 presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s something that\u8217\'20\'19s seven, eight years in the making,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joseph Geevarghese, executive director of the progressive group Our Revolution. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re seeing the fruit of organizing over multiple election cycles.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Our Revolution made 100,000 phone calls and sent 130,000 texts to supporters in St Louis as part of its organizing efforts there, and the group contacted each of its 90,000 Chicago members an average of three times in connection to the mayoral race. The victory in Chicago was particularly meaningful for progressive groups like Our Revolution given that Johnson was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/meetthepressblog/vallas-outspending-johnson-chicago-mayoral-race-rcna74901"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outspent nearly two to one}}}\uc2  on television advertising.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact that we were able to out-organize big money with people power, I think, is significant because that usually does not happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Geevarghese said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it really speaks to the growing sophistication of the progressive movement as a political force.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Another existential election\u8217\'20\'19 on the horizon\par \par \uc2 As Democrats look ahead to 2024, when they will attempt to maintain control of the White House and the Senate while flipping control of the House, progressives say there are some important takeaways to learn from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s results.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s something poetic about the victories in Chicago and Wisconsin taking place because there is a through line there,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cboth around what people want \u8211\'20\'13 which is responsive government, which is an expansion of their freedoms \u8211\'20\'13 and also what the opposition was saying.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mitchell saw \u8220\'20\'1Cfearmongering\u8221\'20\'1D being deployed as a weapon in both Wisconsin and Chicago, particularly around the issue of crime. Vallas, Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s opponent in the mayoral race, received the endorsement of the Fraternal Order of Police and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2023/3/1/23621231/paul-vallas-chicago-mayor-runoff-brandon-johnson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged}}}\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake Chicago the safest city in America\u8221\'20\'1D, as he attacked Johnson over his past support for the defund the police movement. In the end, Johnson won the race {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.wttw.com/2023/04/05/how-brandon-johnson-weathered-attacks-over-claims-he-would-defund-police#:~:text=That%20push%20carried%20Johnson%2C%2047%2C%20to%20victory%20on%20the%20city%E2%80%99s%20South%20and%20West%20sides%2C%20home%20to%20Black%20and%20Latino%20Chicagoans%20who%20live%20in%20neighborhoods%20that%20are%20among%20the%20city%E2%80%99s%20most%20violent%20and%20have%20the%20highest%20rates%20of%20crime."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thanks in part}}}\uc2  to the support of voters living on the city\u8217\'20\'19s South and West sides, which report some of the highest levels of violent crime.\par \par \uc2 Geevarghese argued that Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s win should prompt some reflection for prominent Democrats, including Biden, who seem fearful of attacks over being \u8220\'20\'1Csoft on crime\u8221\'20\'1D. Progressives {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://progressives.house.gov/press-releases?ID=BDDEDE06-DEA7-43A3-9B77-85F477C12EF9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expressed dismay}}}\uc2  last month after Biden signed a Republican bill overturning recent changes to the criminal code of Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were able to talk about a broader vision of community safety, which is having good schools, which is having investment in mental health, which is making sure there\u8217\'20\'19s good jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D Geevarghese said of Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s win.\par \par \uc2 Even before the 2024 elections, progressives will have additional opportunities to demonstrate the effectiveness of their message. Philadelphia will hold its mayoral race in November, and every state legislative seat in Virginia will also be up for grabs this fall. Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin, has called for a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think 2024 is an opportunity for us to learn from this coalition that we built and hopefully replicate it in other places, in other states,\u8221\'20\'1D Mitchell said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to be faced with another existential election on the federal level in not too much time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230408ej480015v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
