\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The dominating issue\u8217\'20\'19: judicial election will decide fate of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in Wisconsin\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman and Sam Levine \par \uc2 1,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2023\par 09:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Control of state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court will ultimately decide fate of 1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that was revived in June, after Roe was overturned\par \par \uc2 One weekend in late March, McKenzie Schroeder offered to drive her friend across the Wisconsin border into Illinois to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been illegal in Wisconsin since June, when the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, reviving the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1849 near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19ve never been in that situation, you can never understand how a woman feels if they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant and don\u8217\'20\'19t know what to do,\u8221\'20\'1D said Schroeder, 30, who lives in Sun Prairie and works for a property management company and as a waitress. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think that any human being on the face of the earth should control what I do with my body.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law has divided voters in the state, who next week could pave the way for getting rid of the ban entirely in the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/13/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-gerrymandered-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consequential election of 2023}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At stake on 4 April is control of the Wisconsin supreme court, which will ultimately decide the fate of the 1849 ban (a challenge is already working its way through state courts). The seven-member supreme court will probably hear consequential cases over voting disputes ahead of the 2024 election in Wisconsin, a key presidential battleground. The outcome of the election could determine whether Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s state legislative districts last for another decade or are replaced. Republicans drew the lines and the districts are so heavily distorted in their favor that it is essentially impossible for Democrats to ever take control of the legislature.\par \par \uc2 That perfect storm of issues has caused a record amount of money \u8211\'20\'13 about $30m \u8211\'20\'13 to flood the race. Daniel Kelly, a conservative, and Janet Protasiewicz, a liberal, are vying to replace the retiring conservative justice Patience Roggensack. Conservatives currently have a 4-3 majority on the state court, so whoever wins the race will determine control of the bench.\par \par \uc2 During the first days of early voting, which began on 21 March, people at the polls across the state cited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and voting rights as well as fair elections as key concerns going into election day. Voters also described crime, a subject that has dominated political ads, as a top concern. The homicide rate in Milwaukee, Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s largest city, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/crime/2023/01/12/milwaukee-sees-overall-violent-crime-drop-7-despite-rise-in-homicides/69801745007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rose}}}\uc2  by 11% in 2022 from the year before, but overall violent crime and other serious offenses dropped by 7%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy number one is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pauline Tanem, a retired foundry worker in Oak Creek. Concerns about democracy and voting rights also informed her support for Protasiewicz. She said she was motivated by \u8220\'20\'1Canything that has to do with voting, and not limiting voting\u8221\'20\'1D, noting that early voting at her polling place closed before 5pm. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople usually work until five.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Barry Burden, a political science professor who closely follows races in Wisconsin and directs the Elections Research Center at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin-Madison\uc2 , said that voters seem to be most interested in a small number of issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the dominating issue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd redistricting and other voting matters are not far behind. Everything else is far down the list.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the Guardian, Protasiewicz pointed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a defining issue of the election, but refrained from calling it the most important one. She has campaigned heavily on her support for a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, though she has said she would decide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues based on existing law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that people are very interested in whether or not they have a right to make their own reproductive healthcare choices,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d be hard pressed to say that it\u8217\'20\'19s a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it\u8217\'20\'19s certainly an issue that concerns people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the race, Kelly has refrained from voicing his opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, although in a since-deleted blogpost he referred to pro-choice organizations and politicians as promoting \u8220\'20\'1Csexual libertinism\u8221\'20\'1D. He has been endorsed by three anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in Wisconsin. (Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s campaign did not respond to requests for an interview.)\par \par \uc2 Political advertising has saturated the airwaves. Protasiewicz has raised a staggering $10m, while both sides have been supported by significant outside spending. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group \uc2 Susan B Anthony\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.bgov.com/news/abortion-ads-help-drive-most-expensive-court-race-in-us-history/?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  spending $2m on the race in support of Kelly, while the advocacy arm of Planned Parenthood has contributed at least $1m in support of Protasiewicz.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPolitical ads are, in my opinion, an unnecessary evil,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Scheuer, an insurance adjuster from Oconomowoc, a heavily Republican city in Waukesha county. \u8220\'20\'1CI think there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of money spent on that that\u8217\'20\'19s wasted.\u8221\'20\'1D Scheuer and his wife, Heather, who works as a secretary at a local Lutheran church, said they were unpersuaded by television advertisements and pointed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the issue driving their support for Kelly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heather Scheuer, who said the issue was a long-term concern and closely tied to her religious beliefs. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are human beings at conception. That\u8217\'20\'19s what we believe in 100%.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Omar Ward, a 26-year-old canvasser with the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ), said voting rights were particularly important to him. Ward, who is from Milwaukee, had his voting rights restored after four years when the state expunged a felony from his record in 2022. His first time casting a ballot since then was in the supreme court primary in February.\par \par \uc2 While canvassing in Milwaukee and Racine, Ward said he heard more about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and crime than other issues. \u8220\'20\'1CNobody feels like they should have to go all the way to Chicago to make a decision on their body and wellbeing,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd on both sides, whether they\u8217\'20\'19re Democrat or Republican, everybody wants the crime to come down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the candidates\u8217\'20\'19 only debate, which was televised on 22 March, Kelly and Protasiewicz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUlapkeqyzI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clashed}}}\uc2  repeatedly over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and safety \u8211\'20\'13 with Kelly casting his opponent as soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz told the Guardian she wanted to push back on that characterization, given \u8220\'20\'1Cthat\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19ve spent my entire career doing, you know, holding people accountable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230403ej43001e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
