\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 She lost her child in a home birth. Prosecutors charged her with murder\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levin in Los Angeles \par \uc2 2,278 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2023\par 10:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The medical examiner ruled the death of Kelsey Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s baby an accident \u8211\'20\'13 yet she faces life in prison: \u8216\'20\'18I mourn every day\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Kelsey Carpenter was alone in her San Diego apartment when she went into labor on 14 November 2020.\par \par \uc2 The mother of two had planned a home birth for her third child. But the baby came two weeks earlier than expected, so she delivered on her own, then passed out, records show. When she awoke, her newborn - whom she named Kiera - was not breathing. Despite her attempts at CPR, the baby did not survive.\par \par \uc2 The loss, however, was only the beginning of Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Police soon after arrested Carpenter, 33. The San Diego district attorney is moving forward with charges of murder \u8220\'20\'1Cwith malice\u8221\'20\'1D and child endangerment and has cited her decision to have an \u8220\'20\'1Cunattended delivery\u8221\'20\'1D as well as her alleged drug use. Prosecutors have continued to pursue the case despite the county medical examiner saying the manner of death was an \u8220\'20\'1Caccident\u8221\'20\'1D; medical experts testifying that the state\u8217\'20\'19s cause-of-death claims were not backed by scientific evidence; and the passage of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2022/09/27/new-protections-for-people-who-need-abortion-care-and-birth-control/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new California law}}}\uc2  explicitly prohibiting the criminalization of pregnancy loss.\par \par \uc2 If convicted, Carpenter could face a life sentence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am still stunned and horrified that a person could have the biggest tragedy of their life and lose a child who was loved and was so wanted, and then be charged with such a horrible crime,\u8221\'20\'1D Carpenter told the Guardian in a recent message from jail. \u8220\'20\'1CI had cherished the idea of this baby and was totally committed to becoming the best mother I knew how to be. I mourn every day for Kiera.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The case comes as women across the US have been increasingly subjected to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/02/renee-bracey-sherman-abortion-rights-activist-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveillance}}}\uc2 , arrest and charges for abortions, stillbirths, and other actions that police claimed \u8220\'20\'1Cendangered\u8221\'20\'1D their fetuses, with routine healthcare decisions and pregnancy outcomes treated as crimes.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s this American idea that we can police our way out of these social problems\par \par \uc2 Dana Sussman, Pregnancy Justice\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  in June 2022, a Nebraska teenager and her mother were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/10/facebook-user-data-abortion-nebraska-police"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged}}}\uc2  with unlawful \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \u8220\'20\'1Cconcealing the death of another person\u8221\'20\'1D after allegedly obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills; a South Carolina woman was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/mar/03/south-carolina-woman-arrested-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arrested after delivering a stillborn}}}\uc2  fetus in a hospital; a pregnant woman found with marijuana was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/sep/12/alabama-jailing-pregnant-marijuana-users-protect-fetuses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jailed for months in Alabama}}}\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect\u8221\'20\'1D her fetus; and three women were sued for wrongful death after a Texas man {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/03/10/texas-abortion-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alleged}}}\uc2  they helped his ex-wife obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s prosecution in California, a state considered a leader on reproductive justice and women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, was a reminder that this crackdown was not limited to conservative states, advocates said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s this American idea that we can police our way out of these social problems \u8211\'20\'13 that placing someone in jail or prison is a way to address mental health needs, substance use disorders or lack of access to healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dana Sussman, acting executive director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pregnancyjusticeus.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pregnancy Justice}}}\uc2 , a legal advocacy group assisting Carpenter. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has really devastating outcomes for everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A tragic \u8216\'20\'18accident\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Carpenter has struggled with substance use disorder for much of her adult life, stemming from sexual abuse and trauma she endured as a child, she said: \u8220\'20\'1CI tried to hide the pain of those experiences in drugs.\u8221\'20\'1D She repeatedly sought treatment and went to rehab, but said that medical professionals who learned of her addiction in some cases responded with punitive measures that caused her life to further unravel.\par \par \uc2 Her first son was born in 2012 and her second in 2019. In both cases, the hospitals reported that the infants tested positive for drugs and called California Child Welfare Services (CWS), which immediately took custody \u8211\'20\'13 despite the fact that both were healthy births, there was no indication either child suffered adverse consequences due to drug exposure, and she was taking prescribed addiction medication, her lawyers say. She has maintained relationships with both kids, with her mother adopting her oldest and Carpenter regaining joint custody of the youngest.\par \par \uc2 When Carpenter became pregnant again with Kiera, she was determined that this time would be different: \u8220\'20\'1CI did not want to risk losing my third child to the system \u8230\'20\'26 I love my babies and hoped to provide a sibling to them. I had imagined my sons being her big brothers and protecting her and showing her love.\u8221\'20\'1D In 2020, she prepared for a home birth, getting literature on delivery at home, reaching out to a midwife, and buying medical supplies, baby care items, a changing table and a crib.\par \par \uc2 She was also in drug treatment again. Records show that in September, she visited a methadone clinic, which prescribes patients medications to manage addiction and withdrawal. The facility was aware she was pregnant and had her take {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/opioids/treatment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 buprenorphine}}}\uc2 , which leading medical {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sitefinitystorage.blob.core.windows.net/sitefinity-production-blobs/docs/default-source/advocacy/2022-sud-pregnant-postpartum.pdf?sfvrsn=61e2c93a_7/%202022-SUD-Pregnant-Postpartum%20.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 associations}}}\uc2  and US health {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://store.samhsa.gov/sites/default/files/d7/priv/sma18-5054.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agencies}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/opioids/treatment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recommended}}}\uc2  for pregnant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.samhsa.gov/newsroom/press-announcements/201802070200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patients}}}\uc2  with opioid use disorder.\par \par \uc2 After delivering her baby, she cut the umbilical cord and secured it with cloth and tape, and also tried unsuccessfully to breastfeed her newborn before losing consciousness amid significant blood loss, her lawyers said. After discovering her baby was lifeless, she attempted CPR and called 911.\par \par \uc2 She was hospitalized \u8211\'20\'13 and then arrested for child endangerment.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Like someone ripped my heart out\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Shande Carpenter, Kelsey\u8217\'20\'19s mother, showed up to the Oceanside police station where her daughter was in shock and severe physical pain while being questioned by officers: \u8220\'20\'1CShe was hysterically crying, collapsing in my arms, completely inconsolable, hyperventilating. She told me Kiera had passed away and the police were blaming her. It was gut-wrenching. I felt like someone was ripping my heart out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She was initially released, but in March 2021, Summer Stephan, the San Diego district attorney, charged Carpenter with murder, prompting local news stories headlined \u8220\'20\'1CDrug addict loses third child\u8221\'20\'1D. She faced significant online abuse, death threats and harassment, including someone spraying \u8220\'20\'1Cbaby killer\u8221\'20\'1D on her car, her mother said: \u8220\'20\'1CI was fearful for her safety, and all of this was happening while she was trying to mourn Kiera.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelsey Carpenter said the stress was overwhelming, and she was consumed by grief: \u8220\'20\'1CBefore I was arrested I slept with Kiera\u8217\'20\'19s ashes next to me every night and told her all of the hopes I had had for us. She was so fully loved for the few minutes I had her. I miss her so much.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The county coroner\u8217\'20\'19s November 2020 autopsy deemed the loss an \u8220\'20\'1Caccident\u8221\'20\'1D, and said the cause was \u8220\'20\'1Cperinatal death associated with methamphetamine and buprenorphine exposure and unattended delivery\u8221\'20\'1D. But a \uc2 Yale University\uc2  expert who reviewed placental records for Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers said a rupture was the most likely cause, meaning blood loss prior to or during delivery, and that there was no evidence that drug exposure was responsible.\par \par \uc2 At a hearing in September 2022, a police detective acknowledged that it was not illegal to have an unattended home birth nor to use buprenorphine. And the medical examiner, when cross-examined by Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s attorney Brian White, acknowledged it was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a homicide because there\u8217\'20\'19s no intent to kill\u8221\'20\'1D, it would be \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Ccertify this death as undetermined\u8221\'20\'1D and it was possible the baby might not have survived if the birth had been at a hospital.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the legality of home birth and uncertainty about the cause of death, Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers grew more confident in their case late last year when lawmakers adopted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aclucalaction.org/bill/ab-2223/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reproductive rights legislation}}}\uc2  that made clear this kind of prosecution simply isn\u8217\'20\'19t allowed.\par \par \uc2 But the new law has not stopped the San Diego DA.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Who do these prosecutions serve?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220AB2223"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AB2223}}}\uc2 , which went into effect in January, states that a person cannot be charged for conduct during pregnancy that results in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , miscarriage, stillbirth or perinatal death. Its adoption came after a district attorney in California\u8217\'20\'19s Central Valley spent years pursuing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/03/california-stillborn-prosecution-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 murder cases against Chelsea Becker and Adora Perez}}}\uc2 , two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-03-18/california-judge-overturns-conviction-woman-whose-baby-was-stillborn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women}}}\uc2  who had stillbirths, alleging {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pregnancyjusticeus.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/prenatal-drug-exposure-12-12.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 without scientific basis}}}\uc2  that drug use caused their losses.\par \par \uc2 Advocates, backed by the state\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/attorney-general-bonta-california-law-does-not-criminalize-pregnancy-loss"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attorney general}}}\uc2 , argued that California\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cmurder of a human fetus\u8221\'20\'1D statute has long only applied to third parties \u8211\'20\'13 if, for instance, someone shoots a pregnant person, leading to a miscarriage. The new law makes this explicit and is supported by medical experts, who argue that criminalizing pregnancy outcomes is unethical, counter to scientific evidence and harmful for public health.\par \par \uc2 Why are we prosecuting tragedies instead of preventing them?\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Chou, \uc2 ACLU\par \par \uc2 Research {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/sites/default/files/publications/files/dapps_fact_sheet_final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shows}}}\uc2  that policies that punish people for substance use during pregnancy can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.apa.org/pi/women/resources/pregnancy-substance-disorders.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lead}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.georgetown.edu/georgetown-law-journal/wp-content/uploads/sites/26/2021/03/Boone_McMichael_State-Created-Fetal-Harm.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worse outcomes}}}\uc2 , in part {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2904854/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 because}}}\uc2  people skip prenatal care and treatment out of fear, said Dr Sarah CM Roberts, \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , professor of obstetrics, gynecology and reproductive sciences: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re prosecuting and blaming people for a decision to avoid care, when we as a society put the policies in place that made them scared to get care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights and health experts who submitted a brief in Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s case also noted that scientific {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21218305/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 studies}}}\uc2  have not demonstrated that a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s use of methamphetamine causes infant death.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s concerning that people who have anything but a perfect birth are vulnerable to this kind of state misinterpretation of the event as willful and the individual therefore culpable for the tragic outcome,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Mishka Terplan, an OB-GYN physician and addiction expert. \u8220\'20\'1CWho do these prosecutions serve? They certainly don\u8217\'20\'19t serve Ms Carpenter. What is to be gained from this?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Chou, ACLU of Northern California staff attorney, said Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s case served to create further mistrust in the medical system: \u8220\'20\'1CWhy are we prosecuting tragedies instead of preventing them? For Ms Carpenter, Ms Perez and Ms Becker, what would have been helpful is connections to resources that they needed to stay healthy, but instead we put them into the criminal system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v Wade was overturned, Pregnancy Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PregnancyJust/status/1521488033727533057"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tracked}}}\uc2  1,700 cases of pregnancy criminalization in the US, many involving substance use claims. While prosecutions of pregnant people suffering from addiction have not typically sparked widespread outrage, Sussman said she hoped people were now paying attention; those cases are the \u8220\'20\'1Cblueprint\u8221\'20\'1D for the broader crackdown on bodily autonomy in a country that increasingly values \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/may/18/abortion-prosecution-fetal-homicide-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fetal personhood}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D and no longer has a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Separated from her kids\par \par \uc2 As Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers have moved to dismiss the case by citing AB2223, San Diego prosecutors have focused on her conduct after the birth, claiming she did not properly secure the umbilical cord, that she took 28 minutes to call 911 after waking up, and that if she had sought help faster, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe baby would have survived\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Tanya Sierra, a DA spokesperson, said in an email that the DA \u8220\'20\'1Cfully recognizes the reproductive rights enshrined by AB 2223\u8221\'20\'1D, but alleged that the new law was not relevant: \u8220\'20\'1CCarpenter is not being prosecuted for her decision to have a home birth or substance use. This is not a case involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stillbirth, or any other pregnancy outcome. This is a case about a newborn baby who died as a direct result of a parent\u8217\'20\'19s acts and omissions after the baby\u8217\'20\'19s live birth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys have countered that there was no clear evidence any of her actions after delivery caused the death and that the DA was still criminalizing her lawful home birth: \u8220\'20\'1CThe law is clear: decisions a woman makes during pregnancy cannot be used to prosecute her for the outcome of the pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniel Arschack.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear if Stephan, the DA, has prosecuted similar cases, which are rare in California. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/columnists/story/2019-10-11/column-d-a-stephan-quits-gop-saying-her-voter-registration-became-a-burden-on-her-job"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 former Republican}}}\uc2 , now a registered independent, Stephan has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sandiegomagazine.com/people/summer-stephan-on-justice-motherhood-and-not-taking-shortcuts/article_08307fde-a914-52f8-9b0f-607f3ce1e615.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presented herself}}}\uc2  as a champion for women and children, and has made national headlines for her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/san-diego-prosecutor-going-after-antifa-once-peddled-antisemitic-george-soros-conspiracies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conspiracy case}}}\uc2  against leftist protesters demonstrating at a pro-Trump rally. Oceanside police did not respond to inquiries. The medical examiner declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Carpenter was originally released on bail, but after a failure to appear in court, she was arrested again and brought to jail in January, her lawyers said, once again separating her from her children.\par \par \uc2 Shande said she wished the DA and the public understood who her daughter really was \u8211\'20\'13 a mother deeply devoted to her sons, who has a gentle spirit. \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s a beautiful, loving, caring, sweet woman, who has been trying so hard for years to heal from the pain that drives her addiction, so she can conquer this disease.\u8221\'20\'1D One of the hardest parts of her incarceration is that her two sons can only see her during brief video visits; the oldest, age 11, cries, saying he misses cuddling and playing with his mom, and the youngest, age three, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t understand why he has to talk to her on a screen.\par \par \uc2 Carpenter had been working to get her real estate license to better support her kids, but she is now unsure she\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to pursue that career, she said, adding that the turmoil of her case had exacerbated her struggles with addiction.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI wish that instead of taking my babies away when I was trying to beat my dependency problem, I could have been supported and been assured that having a baby in a hospital while being treated for dependency did not mean that my baby would be taken away,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI have been a good mother to my children and I would have been to Kiera if I\u8217\'20\'19d had the chance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230403ej43001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
