\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A truly incredible amount of money\u8217\'20\'19: millions ride on one US judicial election\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine \par \uc2 1,054 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2023\par 11:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The race for a place on Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court could have major implications for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , democracy and the 2024 election\par \par \uc2 More than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wispolitics.com/2023/supreme-court-race-spending-tops-37-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $37m}}}\uc2  has already been spent in an election that will this month determine control of Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court, easily making it the most expensive judicial contest in US history.\par \par \uc2 Spending in the race easily shatters the $10m spent in the 2020 Wisconsin supreme court race, the previous record in the state. It also easily surpasses the previous national record, $15m spent on an Illinois supreme court race in 2004. The race has national implications \u8211\'20\'13 it will probably ultimately determine the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state as well as play a key role in setting voting rules for the 2024 election in one of America\u8217\'20\'19s most competitive states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just a truly incredible amount of money,\u8221\'20\'1D said Douglas Keith, a lawyer at the Brennan Center for Justice who closely follows state courts. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a sign of what we should expect to see in the future in other state supreme court elections in other states provided that for some reason a particular seat is seen as important.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A once-in-a-generation set of circumstances have come together to make the state supreme court race between liberal Janet Protasiewicz and conservative Daniel Kelly \u8211\'20\'13 typically a little-noticed contest outside Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s borders \u8211\'20\'13 the most important election this year.\par \par \uc2 First, the ideological balance of the seven-member court is up for grabs. Second, the outcome of the race will probably directly determine whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Wisconsin, as the court is expected to weigh in soon on the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Third, the court could strike down Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s gerrymandered legislative maps, ending Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 unshakable majority in the state. Lastly, the court is expected to weigh in on a range of disputes over election rules ahead of the 2024 presidential election in Wisconsin, a key battleground state.\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz and Kelly have taken different approaches to how that money has been raised. Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s campaign has raised $14.5m in total, a vast haul that dwarfs the $2.7m Kelly has raised. But Kelly has benefited from an influx of outside spending from third-party groups, most notably Fair Courts America, a Super Pac backed by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/21/wisconsin-supreme-court-primary-election-uihlein-family-campaign-finance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GOP mega-donors}}}\uc2  Richard and \uc2 Elizabeth Uihlein\uc2 , which has spent nearly $4.5m on advertising so far. Women Speak Out Pac, which is connected to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America, has also pledged to spend $2m in support of Kelly and has spent nearly $1.3m on advertising so far.\par \par \uc2 The Republican State Leadership Committee (RSLC) \u8211\'20\'13 which focuses on state-level elections \u8211\'20\'13 has also spent about $200,000 in support of Kelly through its Judicial Fairness Initiative, according to an analysis by the Center for Political Accountability, a watchdog group. Some of the RSLC\u8217\'20\'19s donors since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision overturning Roe v Wade have been companies like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2022/digital/news/google-employees-abortion-rights-relocate-1235303248/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Google}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/06/24/tech/companies-abortion-reaction/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Comcast}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/retail-consumer/amazon-reimburse-us-employees-who-travel-treatments-including-abortions-2022-05-02/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  that have pledged to support their employees if they want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the Center for Political Accountability.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have so many major household name companies come out in support of their employees\u8217\'20\'19 access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Offering to cover travel expenses, offering to cover medical expenses, that sort of thing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jeanne Hanna, the Center for Political Accountability\u8217\'20\'19s research director, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut then continuing to fund these groups that elect openly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judges in battleground states where one judicial seat could make the difference of whether people in this state can access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care at all. They\u8217\'20\'19re saying one thing and doing another with their political spending.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelly has openly touted his support from outside groups, telling supporters earlier this month not to worry because a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/15/us/politics/wisconsin-supreme-court.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ccavalry\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  of outside money was coming to support him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat has been most surprising is that Dan Kelly has basically raised no money as a candidate \u8230\'20\'26 So all of his backing has been from outside groups,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barry Burden, the director of the Elections Research Center at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin-Madison\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to understand. Legally, they\u8217\'20\'19re not allowed to coordinate. So he\u8217\'20\'19s essentially handed over messaging to groups that he cannot control.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s fundraising has been prolific. She has spent more than $10.5m on television advertisements alone, compared with Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s $580,000, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/buying-time-2023-wisconsin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a Brennan Center tracker}}}\uc2 . And while she has benefited from considerable spending from liberal outside groups \u8211\'20\'13 A Better Wisconsin Together, \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , and Everytown for Gun Safety among them \u8211\'20\'13 the bulk of the money she\u8217\'20\'19s raised has come from the state Democratic party.\par \par \uc2 The party\u8217\'20\'19s $8.8m contribution to her campaign was made possible by a 2015 Republican rewrite of the state\u8217\'20\'19s campaign finance rules. Those changes removed a cap on the amount of money candidates could receive from state parties. They also allowed individual donors to make unlimited contributions to the political parties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the Republicans rewrote the laws in 2015 \u8230\'20\'26 they did it with the expectation that it would advantage them. They felt that the sources of money they could rely on, both outside groups and big contributors, would mean they would always have financial advantages in races like this. Just the opposite has happened,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Jay Heck\uc2 , the executive director of the Wisconsin chapter of Common Cause, a watchdog group. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the reason why [Wisconsin Democratic party chair] {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/26/ben-wikler-wisconsin-democrats-midterms-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ben Wikler}}}\uc2  and the Democrats have been able to be such a powerhouse.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Protasiewicz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wpr.org/wisconsin-supreme-court-candidate-janet-protasiewicz-recuse-cases-democratic-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has said}}}\uc2  she would recuse herself from cases involving the Wisconsin Democratic party. Kelly has declined to make a similar recusal pledge for cases involving his major donors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJudges should not be able to hear cases involving major donors or supporters,\u8221\'20\'1D said Keith, the Brennan Center expert. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the issues that comes with all this money being as opaque as it is is that the public doesn\u8217\'20\'19t actually know who the judge\u8217\'20\'19s major supporters are often. And if the judges do know, then that\u8217\'20\'19s even more troubling that the judge has information that the public doesn\u8217\'20\'19t about what cases they may have a conflict in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230402ej42000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
