\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 She was one of Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s last  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  doctors. Then they came for everything she had\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 4,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2023\par 17:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dr Leah Torres has endured the ire of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement without backing down \u8211\'20\'13 but now she faces her most daunting challenge\par \par \uc2 Dr Leah Torres doesn\u8217\'20\'19t tell people what she does when she meets them, which makes it hard to make friends. She removes her name from every piece of trash before she puts it out for recycling, in case people walking past see her name and find out where she lives. If a package addressed to her arrives on her porch, she calls everyone she knows to identify who sent it before she opens it \u8211\'20\'13 it could be a bomb.\par \par \uc2 Once, coming back from work in the piercing August Alabama sun, she noticed a gray sedan parked in her driveway. Instinctively, she fled to a neighbor\u8217\'20\'19s house \u8211\'20\'13 she barely knew him \u8211\'20\'13 but asked if he could walk her home anyway. The car turned out to be a stranger\u8217\'20\'19s; the driver had just pulled over to send a text message. \u8220\'20\'1CStill, you never know,\u8221\'20\'1D says Torres, her big, almond-shaped eyes conveying concern.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s not paranoid. Torres is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctor in the state of Alabama, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now illegal except in life-threatening situations. She\u8217\'20\'19s one of the many doctors increasingly targeted by lawmakers, protesters, conservative news outlets and social media, even more so now that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer legal in much of the US.\par \par \uc2 People hate Torres. They have filmed her outside her place of work and posted videos of her online. They approach her sometimes knowing her first name, her car and what time she gets to work in the morning, while she knows nothing about them.\par \par \uc2 When we first meet at the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center in Tuscaloosa, where she works, an anonymous person has just sent her a card with a mirror inside. It reads: \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s a 10. But she needs to find a new job.\u8221\'20\'1D It\u8217\'20\'19s tame in comparison to what some who have worked at the clinic have received in the mail; the former owner {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/independent-abortion-clinic-owners_n_59a56ffae4b0446b3b86750d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  was sent anthrax}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Torres has long been in the public eye. As a proud, loud \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider who is well known on social media, she has made many enemies. But since the state of Alabama took her medical license away, dragging her through a $115,000 legal battle that put her out of work for seven months, fear follows her around, like a stranger ready to snatch her in the night.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 It was 11am on a Thursday when two men presented themselves in the lounge of the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center, asking to see the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s doctor. Torres led the two men down the hall to one of the offices. Inside, the air was stuffy, the windows carpeted by heavy 1940s drapes that deny all sun \u8211\'20\'13 a helpful privacy feature.\par \par \uc2 The men revealed themselves to be investigators sent by the state. One solemnly informed Torres of her predicament: she was being stripped of her license to practice medicine, pending an investigation by the Alabama board of medical examiners.\par \par \uc2 Torres was being accused of fraud. Scott Sides, one of the investigators, informed her she had made errors in her application for a medical license. The hearing was set for 21 December 2020, four months in the future, when she would be able to argue her case. Until then, she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t practice as a doctor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s too far out,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, the first note of panic registering in her otherwise calm demeanor. \u8220\'20\'1CFor my livelihood,\u8221\'20\'1D she clarified, more deep-set in tone.\par \par \uc2 Investigator Sides\u8217\'20\'19 voice sounded gentle, even apologetic, as he informed her that she would have to complain through an attorney if she wanted her hearing moved closer.\par \par \uc2 Torres noticed Sides\u8217\'20\'19 hands \u8211\'20\'13 soft-skinned, supple, a bureaucrat\u8217\'20\'19s hands \u8211\'20\'13 were shaking as he handed her the papers to sign. The whole process struck Torres as strange; she had read about doctors accused of sex offenses in her state who had not been suspended before an investigation confirmed their guilt.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think he knew what he was doing was wrong,\u8221\'20\'1D Torres says now.\par \par \uc2 The officers left, but not before taking Torres\u8217\'20\'19 physical license with them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like a child being reprimanded,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Sides informed the clinic that no patients were to be seen until a covering physician was hired to replace Torres - the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s only doctor - unless her license was reinstated.\par \par \uc2 And so began Torres\u8217\'20\'19 battle with the state, and the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s fight for survival.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 The West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center, a fubsy, brick building bedecked with brown leather armchairs and dark oak cabinets, was \u8211\'20\'13 until relatively recently \u8211\'20\'13 the busiest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in all of Alabama. A two-hour drive from Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s border with Mississippi, its geographic position made it a safe haven for residents traveling from that state , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/issues/reproductive-freedom/abortion/last-clinics-standing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only had one clinic}}}\uc2  serving its childbearing-age population of about 600,000 since 2018.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI assumed most of the patients would be students from the university, seeing as it\u8217\'20\'19s in a university town,\u8221\'20\'1D says Wendy Rawlings, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lithub.com/why-i-became-an-abortion-clinic-escort-at-50/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 volunteered}}}\uc2  as an escort at the clinic between 2017 and 2020. But they weren\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Historically, most of the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s patients have been Black and low-income. Many were mothers unable to take on the financial burden of a new baby (most people who have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/united-states/abortion/demographics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already have children}}}\uc2  ). Less often, they were teenagers who didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to drastically change their life\u8217\'20\'19s course to become a parent.\par \par \uc2 Rawlings\u8217\'20\'19 job was to escort patients from the parking lot to the clinic safely \u8211\'20\'13 a small stretch of land that takes no more than a minute or two to walk across, but a necessary requirement nonetheless. She tried to shield them from the protesters hurling abuse or begging them to change their minds about their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they entered the clinic.\par \par \uc2 As a volunteer, Rawlings met patients who had disabled children and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t take on the extra burden of a new baby; women with chronic pain conditions like sciatica that made it hard to fathom the physical load of chasing a toddler; patients with drug problems who knew birth would land them in jail and their child in foster care; women in violent relationships; rape victims; or people whose pregnancy was a threat to their life for medical reasons.\par \par \uc2 She enjoyed the work. She knew she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t totally shield people from what she calls \u8220\'20\'1Cthe humiliation, the brutality of being lectured through a bullhorn [by protesters] who don\u8217\'20\'19t know you\u8221\'20\'1D. But she sensed she provided a much-needed buffer.\par \par \uc2 Alongside the conviviality between escorts and patients, though, was the harassment. Four years after it opened in 1997, the clinic was subject to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://feminist.org/news/tuscaloosa-al-abortion-clinic-target-of-13th-clinic-arson-bombing-of-1997/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arson attack}}}\uc2 . It was razed to the ground, reopening in the same nondescript business park.\par \par \uc2 The clinic has been threatened countless times, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/independent-abortion-clinic-owners_n_59a56ffae4b0446b3b86750d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even shot at}}}\uc2 . In 2006, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocate drove his Nissan Sentra through the building\u8217\'20\'19s front doors and into the waiting room in the early hours of the morning before patients arrived. The culprit said he didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to harm anyone, although he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tuscaloosanews.com/story/news/2006/04/27/man-charged-in-ramming-of-abortion-clinic/27676277007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 probably wanted to put the clinic out of operation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then came the onslaught of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in Alabama in the late 2000s, which almost shuttered the center. A law requiring hospital permitting privileges for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics closed the facility for the best part of 2018. There were laws that regulated the use of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the clinic, and laws that required a $150,000 ambulance ramp to be fitted to the back of the building (hospitalization is an incredibly rare occurrence).\par \par \uc2 It was a slow, protracted attack by a thousand laws, regulations and expensive emergency fittings. Owner Gloria Gray would often mentally set a date at which time she\u8217\'20\'19d have to close to clinic for good, she explained in a 2016 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2016/03/04/movies/trapped-review-abortion-documentary.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documentary}}}\uc2 . But when that date arrived, she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cmy heart wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let me do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Gray, who was then 69, was ready to retire. In order to do so, she needed someone to buy the clinic. It was a struggle. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are not the most lucrative, nor the easiest, businesses to run, with the onslaught of attacks, the daily gamut of protesters and the ever-shifting rules and regulations.\par \par \uc2 A bill aimed at banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the entire state of Alabama was what finally led to her finding a buyer.\par \par \uc2 Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s Human Life Protection Act \u8211\'20\'13 a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any exceptions \u8211\'20\'13 started making its way into law. In May 2019, the bill passed.\par \par \uc2 The law was unenforceable at the time, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  then remained a federal right. But the first and only outright \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban being passed in the country resulted in a moment of outcry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLiterally from the second the bill was signed, there was so much anger and confusion. Bernie Sanders got {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2019/05/15/bernie-sanders-calls-abortion-a-constitutional-right/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 involved}}}\uc2 . Pussy Riot held an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/b0e81174e4e24e99a733d08f5f0fc7e6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 entire concert}}}\uc2 . Amanda Shires {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2020/09/amanda-shires-sings-about-abortion-problem-for-alabamas-yellowhammer-fund.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote a [song]}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D says Robin Marty, a reproductive rights journalist turned operations director who now raises money for the clinic. The bill resulted in $2m worth of donations to the Yellowhammer fund, a prominent local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund in Alabama that went on to take the clinic off Gray\u8217\'20\'19s hands.\par \par \uc2 And so the clinic survived a little longer. But it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be long before legislators found another angle from which to stop its services.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Two events defined the lives of Torres\u8217\'20\'19 parents, as far as Torres sees it. Her dad, a first-generation American from a Mexican family who grew up in 1940s Michigan, was so tormented by bullies that by the time he was five, he had forced himself to forget how to speak Spanish. Torres, who is adopted, studied languages during her undergraduate studies. She is now a fluent Spanish speaker, but her dad cannot converse with her in his native tongue.\par \par \uc2 When Torres was about eight or nine years old, her mom, a chief nursing officer in a Michigan hospital, fought a sexual harassment case at work. Torres was too young to understand what happened, just that it involved male colleagues using inappropriate language of a sexual nature around her mother, and that after her mom raised the issue, she lost her job. Torres\u8217\'20\'19 mother sued the company, and had to relocate for work.\par \par \uc2 Torres took one lesson from these stories: that at some point in her life she would face discrimination, and that you always fight for what is right.\par \par \uc2 As a kid, she waited for the moment the discrimination would happen. \u8220\'20\'1CI remember being a little girl and being ready. But that just kind of never happened. No boy had the audacity to say to me, \u8216\'20\'18Girls can\u8217\'20\'19t play baseball.\u8217\'20\'19 It was always more subtle than that,\u8221\'20\'1D says Torres.\par \par \uc2 She decided she was going to become an OB-GYN in college after learning about a study on uterine cancer in which all of the subjects were men.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCue the laughter,\u8221\'20\'1D says Torres, rolling her eyes, as she does frequently. \u8220\'20\'1CI was just like, \u8216\'20\'18Well, shit!\u8217\'20\'19 I mean, I knew women\u8217\'20\'19s health was underserved \u8230\'20\'26 But that\u8217\'20\'19s when I became fascinated with historical misogyny, specifically in medicine. And I said, \u8216\'20\'18Well, I gotta change that.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Being an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider felt like a good place to start. In 2012, she was sent to Utah so she could further specialise through a fellowship, which is where she started to develop her activist chops.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was doing abortions in a red state. You\u8217\'20\'19re fighting all these legislators \u8230\'20\'26 It was a high anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill time,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, describing a fetal pain bill that she fought tooth and nail to keep off the books during the fellowship.\par \par \uc2 On the wall of her office hangs a cartoon by the Salt Lake Tribune\u8217\'20\'19s Pat Bagley, in which he depicts Torres fighting the bill. The cartoon shows a Utah legislator giving a medical demonstration while using the children\u8217\'20\'19s game {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/search?q=operation+game&tbm=isch&chips=q:operation+game,g_1:body:B7qqXgkx7F4%3D&rlz=1C5CHFA_enUS986US986&hl=en-GB&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjykdiK0bv9AhVxBFkFHX1FCosQ4lYoBXoECAEQMA&biw=1425&bih=821#imgrc=yK9GQlRjRsudRM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Operation}}}\uc2  and preaching to the doctors. \u8220\'20\'1CAt no point in the procedure must the metal thingy touch the other metal thingy inside the woman\u8217\'20\'19s thingy,\u8221\'20\'1D the legislator explains.\par \par \uc2 The meeting never took place \u8211\'20\'13 the cartoon is satire. But Torres\u8217\'20\'19 portrayal, her eyes deeply embedded in the back of her eyeball sockets, groaning in despair, is true to life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLegislators are notoriously not trained in medicine. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s appropriate they\u8217\'20\'19re tying our hands and dictating how to provide care to those of us who are trained and licensed,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 With her patients, Torres is very different from the argumentative doctor depicted in cartoons or on social media. She has a gentle demeanor. She leans back in her seat, her clogs neatly poking out from her scrubs, while she explains STI care, or takes time to discuss different birth control options. She lets patients know their rights \u8211\'20\'13 which includes their right not to take a single bit of her advice if they do not want to. She spent most of 2021, during her license suspension, updating the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s administrative practices to ensure no patient is turned away from care because of financial issues.\par \par \uc2 Her reason is simple.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I don\u8217\'20\'19t do this work, who\u8217\'20\'19s going to?\u8221\'20\'1D she asks.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 By the time Torres was in her 30s, the fight she had long been anticipating finally found her. On the way to a conference in Australia, someone sent her an antagonizing tweet, asking her if she heard the screams of the fetuses she aborts.\par \par \uc2 She hastily fired off a response.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou know fetuses can\u8217\'20\'19t scream, right? I transect the cord 1st so there\u8217\'20\'19s really no opportunity, if they\u8217\'20\'19re even far enough along to have a larynx. I won\u8217\'20\'19t apologize for performing medicine. I\u8217\'20\'19m also a \u8216\'20\'18uterus ripper outer,\u8217\'20\'19 if that\u8217\'20\'19s how you\u8217\'20\'19d like to describe hysterectomy,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote.\par \par \uc2 By the time she\u8217\'20\'19d landed, it had gone viral. Former Breitbart editor Ben Shapiro shared the tweet, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/benshapiro/status/973763828117745665?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E973763828117745665%7Ctwgr%5E83cf7511088fd1bc0dddeb61beb7be6662f5679a%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fdailycaller.com%2F2018%2F03%2F14%2Fben-shapiro-abortion-tweet%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comparing Torres to a James Bond villain}}}\uc2 . Conservative news outlets like the Daily Caller covered it, misinterpreting it to read that Torres cut vocal cords (which is not part of any standard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure), when she meant the umbilical cord. People started pulling out of the Australian conference at which Torres was scheduled to speak.\par \par \uc2 When I ask Torres about this incident, multiple times over several days, she is conflicted. Often, she shrugs it off, and many times she tells me she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t regret sending the tweet.\par \par \uc2 She was incensed, she says, that the US debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had come to revolve so much around humanizing the fetus, and not the person carrying the fetus. For her, it was obvious that she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t going around cutting vocal cords. She was angry that bad faith actors seemed to be suggesting \u8211\'20\'13 to her, a doctor \u8211\'20\'13 that her job involved murdering screaming babies.\par \par \uc2 She had come to view the reaction to her tweet as almost inevitable: at some point, rightwing pundits and commentators were going to find some way to publicly demonize and flog her.\par \par \uc2 I tell her the tweet might read as harsh, unthinking even \u8211\'20\'13 and not just to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKnowing what transpired, I would have worded it differently. There was more of an emotional reaction in my post than normal,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CPart of me wonders, why did I even respond? Normally I don\u8217\'20\'19t respond to nonsense like that. I think I did because I\u8217\'20\'19d been punched in the face so many times. I was like, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m done getting punched in the face.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Torres compares herself to Bruce Banner, the nerdy physicist whose alter ego is the Hulk, a raging brute with superhuman strength. When I ask her why it took her so long to see why people reacted so strongly to her tweet, she softens. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m always angry. I think I\u8217\'20\'19m in a state of anger and rage. And I won\u8217\'20\'19t allow myself to be vulnerable at times,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 After her viral moment, Torres deleted the tweet. The next week, she was on a flight back to Utah. A few days later, after having been directed not to come into work, she was called to a meeting in a generic-looking conference room she\u8217\'20\'19d never been to before, on the edge of town. An HR person she didn\u8217\'20\'19t know handed her a mutual separation agreement and told her: sign it and you\u8217\'20\'19ll get three months\u8217\'20\'19 severance, or don\u8217\'20\'19t and leave without pay.\par \par \uc2 She was later chaperoned into her old office to collect her belongings \u8211\'20\'13 a mortifying moment in which not a single colleague would look her in the eye. \u8220\'20\'1CIt felt like I was a criminal. Like I had laundered money or something,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalls.\par \par \uc2 Torres would later sue the Daily Caller for defamation, alleging it had misinterpreted her tweet. The article written about her, and many since, indeed claimed she was referring to vocal cords, which are plural, and not what Torres says she was referencing.\par \par \uc2 She was awarded $40,000 in damages, and the outlet issued a correction, but she never received an apology. After that, Torres couldn\u8217\'20\'19t find full-time work for a year, until she was offered a job in New Mexico. She refers to that time in her life as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccorporate medicine nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She held on for a year, until Robin Marty called her in March 2020 to ask if she would be interested in being the main doctor at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in a different state. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t even wait to hear where the clinic was before she said yes.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Torres was barely practicing in Alabama for two weeks before the state took away her license. Bobby Segall, whom she describes as a \u8220\'20\'1Crootin\u8217\'20\'19 tootin\u8217\'20\'19 Alabama lawyer\u8221\'20\'1D, with a moustache like Albert Einstein and a head of thick white hair, took on Torres\u8217\'20\'19 case against the Alabama state medical board.\par \par \uc2 In deposition, he guided Torres to remain polite and patient as the state listed its charges against her: mainly fraud, for allegedly incorrectly answering statements on her application for a medical license. Torres was accused of lying about a medical malpractice case; lying about her mental health; lying about having her staff privileges revoked; giving incorrect dates of employment; and lying about intending to treat Covid-19 patients.\par \par \uc2 Another accusation was added later: that she had made \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic statements related to the practice of medicine which violate the high standards of honesty, diligence, prudence, and ethical integrity demanded from physicians licensed to practice in Alabama\u8221\'20\'1D. Torres and her lawyer had no doubt this was related to her 2018 tweet.\par \par \uc2 Torres was not found to have committed any wrongdoing in the malpractice case mentioned, and the issue was settled out of court. As for her 2018 tweet, she stated: \u8220\'20\'1C[My] comments were deliberately misconstrued by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists \u8230\'20\'26 These activists and certain media figures fanned the flames of this false controversy until I became the subject of serious harassment and threats on the internet and through calls to my workplace.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And then Torres waited. She paid her legal fees in dribs and drabs as the invoices piled up. She was always in the red, but promised the lawyers she was good for the money. She made a point of annotating the margins of the thin white paper bills in her messy, compact scrawl, trying to keep track of what was coming and going.\par \par \uc2 Seven months later, her license was given back, with insufficient evidence found that she had violated any standards.\par \par \uc2 The committee who reviewed the case did not endorse the board\u8217\'20\'19s allegations against Torres, although the report concluded that elements of her answers \u8220\'20\'1Cwere suggestive of deceptive answers and a lack of ethical integrity expected of practicing physicians in Alabama\u8221\'20\'1D. As a result, she was instructed to take an ethics course and charged $4,000 in administrative fees to the board.\par \par \uc2 When the case was removed from the national databank that holds information on medical malpractice against doctors, the following explanation was soberly put: \u8220\'20\'1CThe action was reversed because the original action should never have been taken.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While the Alabama state medical board gave Torres her license back, it never repaid her legal costs, nor did it compensate her for the months of wages she had to forgo during the seven months it took to reach a verdict on her case. The fees totalled $115,360.93.\par \par \uc2 Marty fundraised to pay almost half of Torres\u8217\'20\'19 fees using donations from the public, and Torres\u8217\'20\'19 savings were used to deal with the rest. The emotional, financial and physical toll on her has been huge.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, the treatment of other doctors since Torres\u8217\'20\'19 temporary suspension might put any doctor off becoming an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. Dr Caitlin Bernard, an OB-GYN in Indiana who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/dr-caitlin-bernard-lawsuit-indiana-attorney-general-abortion-10-year-old-rape-victim/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went public}}}\uc2  about a 10-year-old rape victim traveling to her state for her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to escape Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s ban, is also currently under investigation by her state\u8217\'20\'19s medical board. She was first accused of lying, and then of breaking doctor-patient confidentiality laws when she chose to speak out. Bernard denies this.\par \par \uc2 From where they stand, neither Bernard nor Torres have been accused because they broke a law or endangered a patient. One thing singled each of them out: how loudly they were arguing against the destruction of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Rawlings, the former clinic escort, drives past the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center almost every day on her way to work. These days, the sight makes her feel she has lost a long battle. There used to be white poles erected outside the clinic to keep protesters within a designated area. Now they are gone, alongside the many people she usually saw outside the clinic, come rain, shine or pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the past, I would drive by and see friends I had volunteered with, sitting out there on lawn chairs. Back then, there was struggle, but you got a sense that we were winning,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow the place is just empty.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t miss the protesters, she says, but their absence is the ultimate signal that they won.\par \par \uc2 With the seven months of closure that came after Torres\u8217\'20\'19 license suspension, the clinic was fast in the red, and had to lay off a slew of staff.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen months later on 24 June 2022, Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s Human Life Protection Act was finally able to come into effect after Roe v Wade was overturned. Now a busy day at the clinic might mean seeing seven patients, though the clinic previously saw sometimes a hundred patients in a week.\par \par \uc2 Marty, the operations director, believes Torres\u8217\'20\'19 license suspension was just another way to prevent abortions from taking place in Alabama, before the overturning of Roe put most abortions fully on hold for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 But Torres\u8217\'20\'19 predicament might become an increasingly common reality for doctors who continue to provide abortions in conservative states \u8211\'20\'13 where people will continue to need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the circumstances they are allowed to \u8211\'20\'13 like when they spontaneously miscarry, or go to the ER with life-threatening complications.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what bullying looks like. This is the mob. The far-right, theocratic, Christian nationalist mob,\u8221\'20\'1D says Marty. She pauses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what [anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] states do. And Alabama hates \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is a tactic that is going to be used on other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in hospitals, in states that still have care. Everything that happens on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , happens in Alabama first,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 In Marty\u8217\'20\'19s view, the state was always looking for a way to be able to shut down the clinic, knowing that it was the most productive one in the state. \u8220\'20\'1CThey figure out the one pressure point that has the most power over an entire state. Their entire mission is how to hold the most power with the least amount of people,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 They may have finally found that pressure point.\par \par \uc2 Asked how long the clinic can hold on financially before it has to close, Torres estimates until maybe June.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230322ej3m00131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
