\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How close to death must a woman be to get an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in Tennessee?\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner \par \uc2 1,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2023\par 16:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the US doesn\u8217\'20\'19t allow exceptions for medical emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 and efforts to change it face powerful opposition from the right\par \par \uc2 Months after the implementation of the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/22/diary-doctor-navigating-total-abortion-ban-tennessee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stringent \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban in the country}}}\uc2 , conservative lawmakers in Tennessee have publicly acknowledged that the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban poses grave risks to the lives of women.\par \par \uc2 Now a political debate over how to change the law is centered on questions that would have been considered unthinkable before last June\u8217\'20\'19s reversal of Roe v Wade: like how close to death a woman must be before a doctor may legally treat her if it means terminating her pregnancy, and whether women should be forced to carry embryos with fatal anomalies to term.\par \par \uc2 Will Brewer, the powerful lobbyist of Tennessee Right to Life, a Christian anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that wrote the current ban, has been accused of waging a campaign of intimidation against lawmakers who he has said are seeking to \u8220\'20\'1Cweaken\u8221\'20\'1D the law. In public testimony and private meetings, Brewer has said women should only be offered terminations if they are facing acute emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 such as when they enter an emergency room \u8220\'20\'1Cbleeding out\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and suggested some complications can \u8220\'20\'1Cwork themselves out\u8221\'20\'1D without medical intervention.\par \par \uc2 Speaking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ehClefotPmc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last week before the West Knoxville Republican Club}}}\uc2 , Brewer also questioned the veracity of medical diagnoses involving what is known as lethal fetal anomalies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWho\u8217\'20\'19s to say with any kind of certainty what a medically futile pregnancy is or a fatal fetal anomaly, which is some condition with the baby that will not allow it to live outside of the womb?\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The political debate is expected to come to a head this week, when two competing proposals \u8211\'20\'13 one of them endorsed by \uc2 Brewer\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 will face votes in the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-controlled legislature.\par \par \uc2 The fractious political fights shows how radically the battle lines over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have shifted since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision last June to reverse Roe v Wade, which in effect allowed every state to determine its own rules. In Tennessee, one of 13 states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently banned, a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger law\u8221\'20\'1D came into effect that had been passed in 2019 in the event that Roe was ever overturned. The law states that any doctor who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committing a felony, and must defend themselves in court in order to prove the procedure was done to save the life of the pregnant patient.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, doctors who spoke with the Guardian on the condition of anonymity say anecdotal evidence suggests the state is already seeing an uptick in teenage births and that \u8211\'20\'13 since it has been about nine months since the trigger ban took effect \u8211\'20\'13 they are anticipating an increased number of babies being born who have life-threatening conditions that will immediately require treatment in neonatal units.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m anticipating those situations to be among people without resources to go out of state\u8221\'20\'1D for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, one doctor said.\par \par \uc2 At least one Republican lawmaker, state senator Richard Briggs, who is also a heart surgeon, has suggested that he co-sponsored the trigger bill without fully understanding its implications. Briggs has proposed a new law so that doctors \u8211\'20\'13 using their \u8220\'20\'1Cgood faith judgment\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 could perform abortions to prevent or treat medical emergencies. It also allows women to terminate pregnancies if the fetus is not viable.\par \par \uc2 Briggs said he is still vehemently opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in an interview, he said the current ban posed risks to the lives of women and girls, and cited a conversation he had with one obstetrician who told him about two pregnant patients she had seen since July who were just 12 years old.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA fifth-grader \u8230\'20\'26 their bodies are not prepared to carry a pregnancy to term. There is irreparable damage that can be done that will render it impossible for her to have children in the future. That is what Right to Life does,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in reference to the group that wrote the law.\par \par \uc2 While the law proposed by Briggs in Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s senate, and its companion legislation in the house, seemed to initially gain traction, Tennessee Right to Life has staunchly opposed it, and threatened to revoke its support for any Republican who supports it. It has already \u8220\'20\'1Cun-endorsed\u8221\'20\'1D Briggs.\par \par \uc2 A legal memo obtained by the Guardian that was written for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group by \uc2 Paul Linton\uc2 , a longtime advocate in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, raised objections to the proposal for allowing medical interventions to \u8220\'20\'1Cprevent\u8221\'20\'1D emergencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is objectionable because, without requiring an existing condition that would qualify under the definition of \u8216\'20\'18medical emergency\u8217\'20\'19, a physician could perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \u8216\'20\'18prevent\u8217\'20\'19 a condition that might not ever materialize,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the Guardian, Linton denied his stance could lead to care being delayed. Asked about the Right to Life\u8217\'20\'19s insistence that women be forced to carry fetuses with lethal anomalies to term, he compared those fetuses to prisoners on death row, saying that just because they were expected to die did not mean a fellow prisoner could \u8211\'20\'13 prematurely \u8211\'20\'13 murder them.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee Right to Life has recently endorsed its own alternative legislation to counter the Briggs proposal. Its proposed bill would allow doctors to perform abortions if a woman has already miscarried or has an ectopic pregnancy, and states that doctors could only perform terminations if required in an acute emergency determined by their \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable medical judgment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Chloe Akers, a Tennessee criminal defense attorney, said the legal standards included in the Right to Life proposal would, in effect, make it easier for prosecutors to convict doctors. This, in turn, could worsen the chilling effect currently preventing care, resulting in even more delays. No doctor has been prosecuted under the current ban.\par \par \uc2 The reality is you cannot ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep women safe\par \par \uc2 Attorney Chloe Akers\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis proposal will result in significantly more delays and denials of care than Briggs\u8217\'20\'19s bill,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is you cannot ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep women safe,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 For Allie Phillips, the stark reality of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban hit her late last month, after she was told at 19 weeks of pregnancy that her baby was not viable and that continuing to carry it posed risks to her health. Instead of being offered a termination, Phillips was forced to look out of state for treatment, eventually traveling to New York City. The cost of the trip and procedure \u8211\'20\'13 which was not covered by her Tennessee insurance plan \u8211\'20\'13 was ultimately paid for with the help of a public appeal for financial help over \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t wrap my mind around the fact that not only is it men making laws against women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies, but that a politician has the right to tell you what you can and can\u8217\'20\'19t do medically \u8211\'20\'13 especially in 2023,\u8221\'20\'1D Phillips said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can be mad about it, I can cry about it. At the end of the day this is the most traumatic thing that has happened to me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One Tennessee obstetrician who spoke on the condition of anonymity described how patient care had been delayed since the implementation of the statewide ban, including for pregnant patients facing acute emergencies caused by issues such as infection or their water breaking prematurely.\par \par \uc2 In one case, the obstetrician said, a pregnant patient brought to a hospital by an ambulance who required an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 18 weeks faced a delay in care inside the hospital because of bureaucratic hurdles.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was all the additional paperwork and calling legal and making sure that anesthesia and nursing teams all understood that we were about to commit a felony, and then making sure we have a team that is comfortable with that,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctor said.\par \par \uc2 In another case, a patient whose pregnancy faced serious complications \u8211\'20\'13 the fetus had a heartbeat but there was no amniotic fluid \u8211\'20\'13 needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 13 weeks but was sent home. She returned a week later showing signs of infection and needed four units of blood.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t seem like a great use of limited resources when there was no chance that pregnancy was going to survive,\u8221\'20\'1D the obstetrician said.\par \par \uc2 Got a tip on this story? Please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Stephanie.Kirchgaessner@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stephanie.Kirchgaessner@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230320ej3k00131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
