\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Wyoming becomes first US state to outlaw use of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2023\par 10:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bill from Republican-controlled legislature comes as measures to crack down on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills gather pace across the country\par \par \uc2 Wyoming has become the first US state to outlaw the use or prescription of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills after the governor, \uc2 Mark Gordon\uc2 , signed into law a bill that was passed by the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-controlled legislature earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The crux of the two-page Wyoming bill is a provision making it illegal to \u8220\'20\'1Cprescribe, dispense, distribute, sell or use any drug for the purpose of procuring or performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 So-called \u8220\'20\'1Cmorning-after\u8221\'20\'1D pills, prescription contraceptive medication used after sex but before a pregnancy can be confirmed, are exempted from the ban.\par \par \uc2 The measure also includes an exemption for any treatment necessary to protect a woman \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom an imminent peril that substantially endangers her life or health\u8221\'20\'1D, as well as any treatment of a \u8220\'20\'1Cnatural miscarriage according to currently accepted medical guidelines\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Violation of the ban is to be treated as a criminal misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail and a fine of up to $9,000.\par \par \uc2 The measure stipulates that a woman \u8220\'20\'1Cupon whom a chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed or attempted shall not be criminally prosecuted\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Wyoming ACLU advocacy director Antonio Serrano criticised the governor\u8217\'20\'19s decision to sign the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA person\u8217\'20\'19s health, not politics, should guide important medical decisions \u8211\'20\'13 including the decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Serrano said.\par \par \uc2 The governor said he was also allowing enactment, without his signature, of a separate bill passed by state lawmakers to prohibit conventional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures except when necessary to protect the health and life of the mother, or in case of rape or incest. Exception is also permitted to end a pregnancy if doctors determine there to be a lethal abnormality of the foetus.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming\u8217\'20\'19s new law comes as a rightwing push to crack down on medication abortions gathers momentum, with a federal judge in Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/15/texas-abortion-pill-ban-judge-kacsmaryk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently considering a nationwide ban}}}\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone in response to a lawsuit by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups.\par \par \uc2 A two pill combination of mifepristone and another drug is the most common form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 Medication abortions were the preferred method for ending pregnancy in the US even before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the ruling that protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for nearly five decades.\par \par \uc2 Since that decision last June, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have been up to states and the landscape has shifted quickly. Thirteen states are now enforcing bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any point in pregnancy, and one more, Georgia, bans it once cardiac activity can be detected, or at about six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation.\par \par \uc2 Courts have put on hold enforcement of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or deep restrictions in Arizona, Indiana, Montana, Ohio, South Carolina, Utah and Wyoming. Idaho courts have forced the state to allow abortions during medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Reuters and Associated Press contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230320ej3k001mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
