\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Election-denying donors pour millions into key Wisconsin supreme court race\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman \par \uc2 1,191 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2023\par 13:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18An organized group of insurrectionists\u8217\'20\'19 is seeking to swing a vote with big implications for voting rights, redistricting and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 More than $3.9m has poured into the Wisconsin supreme court election from individuals and groups involved with promoting election disinformation and attempts to overturn the 2020 election, according to an analysis of campaign spending by the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The contributions, in support of the conservative candidate Daniel Kelly, come amid a race that has broken national campaign spending records. According to a campaign finance tracker by the Brennan Center for Justice, political ad orders for the liberal county judge Janet Protasiewicz and conservative Kelly have reached at least $20m in anticipation of the 4 April general election.\par \par \uc2 The Wisconsin supreme court is currently made up of three judges who lean liberal and four conservatives. Whoever replaces the conservative retiring justice Patience Roggensack will determine the ideological composition of the court, which has been dominated by the right wing for 15 years. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/13/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-gerrymandered-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At stake}}}\uc2  in the Wisconsin supreme court race are redistricting, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and voting rights and elections policy. And these decisions go beyond the state: Wisconsin has been a critical swing state in recent presidential elections, so its voting policies affect more than just state residents.\par \par \uc2 Among the election-denying funders behind Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s run are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinktanks and Super Pacs, conservative billionaires and a constellation of groups funded by the Wisconsin shipping supply tycoon \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, prominent election denier Charlie Kirk of the rightwing Turning Point USA has voiced support for Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s campaign. Kirk has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/jan-6-organizer-ali-alexander-blames-turning-point-usa-for-capitol-riot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed}}}\uc2  for funding the travel of a large number of the Capitol rioters on January 6.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is an organized group of insurrectionists who are trying to actively fund candidates for elected office, including judgeships,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jeremi Suri, a professor of public affairs and history at the \uc2 University of Texas-Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The funding comes after prominent election deniers lost their efforts in the 2022 midterms to oversee elections in swing states including Arizona, Michigan and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the insurrectionists have given up,\u8221\'20\'1D Suri said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re still looking for candidates, and they\u8217\'20\'19re particularly interested in someone like Kelly, who, if he can get on the supreme court, will have a 10-year term and will be pretty much unaccountable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Looming behind the biggest campaign splurges is \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/21/wisconsin-supreme-court-primary-election-uihlein-family-campaign-finance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heir}}}\uc2  to the Schlitz beer fortune and founder of Uline, a shipping supply company based in Wisconsin. Uihlein has gained notoriety for his financial support of groups involved with the 6 January 2021 Capitol riot and thinktanks that stoked unfounded fears of election fraud during and following the 2020 election \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/new-filings-reveal-another-billionaire-dick-uihlein-behind-the-big-lie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including}}}\uc2  the Conservative Partnership Institute and the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Campaign finance reports show the group Fair Courts America, Inc, which is largely bankrolled by Uihlein, spent at least $1.5m in independent expenditures on pro-Kelly television ads before the 21 February primary. According to the Brennan Center for Justice, Fair Courts America has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/buying-time-2023-wisconsin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 booked}}}\uc2  more than $3.9m in ads scheduled to run by 4 April.\par \par \uc2 Uihlein\u8217\'20\'19s influence in the Wisconsin supreme court race extends beyond Fair Courts America. Restoration Pac, a funding vehicle that Uihlein has poured millions into, finances other pro-Kelly groups, including Women Speak Out Pac. Reports show Women Speak Out Pac has spent more than $100,000 on the primary and general election so far.\par \par \uc2 American Principles Project Pac, which has reported contributing more than $66,000 on pro-Kelly digital advertising before the primary, is also funded by Restoration Pac.\par \par \uc2 Although Uihlein dollars figure prominently in the Wisconsin supreme court race, the billionaire is one of multiple mega-donors behind Restoration Pac. The Florida private equity billionaire John Childs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fec.gov/data/committee/C00571588/?tab=raising"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contributed}}}\uc2  at least $800,000 to the Pac in 2022, according to filings with the Federal Elections Commission (\uc2 FEC\uc2 ). Childs has also contributed to America First Action, the Super Pac that supported Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 run for office.\par \par \uc2 The Texas billionaire Brett Hendrickson, who has contributed to the campaigns of the Missouri congressman Eric Burlison, Senator \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  of North Carolina and the Illinois congresswoman Mary Miller, has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fec.gov/data/committee/C00544387/?tab=raising&cycle=2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contributed}}}\uc2  to Restoration Pac. Burlison, Budd and Miller have all aligned themselves with Trump in questioning the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 According to campaign finance filings, the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  chairman, \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , contributed $20,000 to Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s campaign \u8211\'20\'13 the maximum amount an individual donor can contribute to a supreme court campaign in Wisconsin. In addition to his work with the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , Leo has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2020/01/07/leonard-leo-crc-advisors-federalist-society"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ties}}}\uc2  to the Concord Fund and the 85 Fund, groups that have pushed the widely disputed independent state legislature theory that claims state legislatures have full jurisdiction to conduct federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Also donating the maximum $20,000 to Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s campaign was \uc2 Diane Hendricks\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/profile/diane-hendricks/?sh=4d0b44875c8a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chairwoman}}}\uc2  of the multibillion-dollar building company ABC Supply. According to the \uc2 FEC\uc2 , Hendricks has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fec.gov/data/receipts/individual-contributions/?contributor_name=Diane+Hendricks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 donated}}}\uc2  at least $4m to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 America First Pac, and more than $500,000 to Trump Victory and Make America Great Again, Again! Inc.\par \par \uc2 The deluge of cash from the same groups that financially supported election denialism and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s failed bid to overturn the 2020 election also played an outsize role during the 2022 elections. Billionaire venture capitalist \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  and Trump-allied conservative strategist Larry Ellison, for example, reportedly spent more than $40m during the 2022 election cycle.\par \par \uc2 A bombshell {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2023/02/17/wisconsin-supreme-court-candidate-dan-kelly-was-paid-120000-by-gop/69912903007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  in the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel raised questions about Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s own participation in 2020 attempts to overturn the presidential election. Kelly, the report found, had provided special counsel to the Republican party, advising on the subject of fake electors. He has also been paid close to $120,000 for his work on \u8220\'20\'1Celection integrity\u8221\'20\'1D by the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and state GOP.\par \par \uc2 Given that the winner of the supreme court race will help decide the future of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Wisconsin, money has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-biden-politics-wisconsin-state-government-2f7181886af566e3c51613389df462e0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poured}}}\uc2  into the race on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight. Planned Parenthood Pacs promised more than $1m for Protasiewicz and the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Women Speak Out Pac has spent at least $100,000 on pro-Kelly materials. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and funders behind Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s campaign have also taken part in efforts to cast doubt on the 2020 election results \u8211\'20\'13 highlighting the common cause rightwing Christian activists have found with election deniers.\par \par \uc2 On a 21 February episode of the Charlie Kirk Show, Julaine Appling \u8211\'20\'13 president of the Christian, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wisconsin Family Council \u8211\'20\'13 endorsed Kelly while invoking fears of electoral fraud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust take the ballot harvesting,\u8221\'20\'1D said Appling. \u8220\'20\'1CThat was something that the liberals were pushing and pushing and doing these bizarre things during the 2020 election that were clearly outside the law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Family Action, Inc, which Appling also heads, has reported about $6,000 in independent expenditures to support Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 Kirk, who founded Turning Point USA and played a prominent role in mobilizing protestors for rallies that preceded the insurrection at the US Capitol on January 6, concurred.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDan Kelly is the best of all of them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230314ej3e001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
