\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Five women denied  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care in Texas sue state over bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par 19:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The women were refused abortions despite carrying nonviable pregnancies that posed major risks to their health\par \par \uc2 Five women denied abortions in Texas, along with two doctors, have sued the state after they were refused \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care despite suffering severe complications with their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 None of the plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 fetuses had a chance of survival. The state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are supposed to allow for the procedure in cases where there is a fatal diagnosis for the fetus, as well as when the pregnancy poses substantial harms to the pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 And yet, under the overlapping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect in Texas \u8211\'20\'13 which threaten doctors with losing their medical licenses, hundreds of thousands of dollars in fines and up to 99 years in jail for providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 the plaintiffs claim they were not given the healthcare they needed and were entitled to.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are scared and outraged at the thought of being pregnant in this state,\u8221\'20\'1D Amanda Zurawski, one of the plaintiffs in the case said on Tuesday in a press conference about the lawsuit in front of the Texas capitol in Austin.\par \par \uc2 According to the Center for Reproductive Rights, which is bringing the lawsuit, the case is the first since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade last year in which pregnant women are challenging the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that are wreaking havoc on reproductive health in much of the country. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want the state of Texas to acknowledge the women behind me should have received timely \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Molly Duane, the lead litigator in the case, outside the Texas capitol. She said the lawsuit aimed to establish that what happened to the plaintiffs was a violation of their basic human rights according to the Texas constitution.\par \par \uc2 The women named in the case encountered a range of different outcomes and treatment detailed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Zurawski-v-State-of-Texas-Complaint.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a 92-page complaint}}}\uc2  filed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski nearly lost her life before doctors would intervene, the lawsuit claims, even though doctors long knew the pregnancy would not be viable. After becoming pregnant following 18 months of fertility treatments, Zurawski was diagnosed in her 17th week with a weakened cervix. While she was told there was no chance the fetus would survive, she was denied treatment in Texas until she had gone into septic shock, with a high fever, and delivered a stillbirth.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, according to the complaint, Zurawski ended up in the ICU following a secondary infection and septic shock, and \u8220\'20\'1CAmanda\u8217\'20\'19s family flew to Austin from across the country because they worried it would be the last time they would see her\u8221\'20\'1D. Zurawski is left with just one functioning fallopian tube. She is currently undergoing a fresh round of IVF treatments.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe preventable harm inflicted on me will make it harder than it already was for me to get pregnant again,\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski said at the press conference. \u8220\'20\'1CThese bans are having real effects on real people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The other women traveled hundreds of miles out of state for abortions, sometimes risking going into septic shock on the plane, and traveling as far as Seattle and Colorado to receive care. During the press conference, one of the plaintiffs, Lauren Hall, detailed having to walk past protesters accusing her of killing her baby after traveling out of state for care for her unviable pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Zurawski and Hall, the women bringing the case are Lauren Miller, Anna Zargarian and Ashley Brandt. They are joined by two doctors, Damla Karsan and Judy Levison, both obstetricians-gynaecologists with experience in providing abortions, who argue that their medical practice has been chilled by the bans.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who is named as a defendant in the case, said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CAttorney General Paxton is committed to doing everything in his power to protect mothers, families and unborn children, and he will continue to defend and enforce the laws duly enacted by the Texas legislature.\u8221\'20\'1D The spokesperson also attached the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://texasattorneygeneral.gov/sites/default/files/images/executive-management/Updated%20Post-Roe%20Advisory%20Upon%20Issuance%20of%20Dobbs%20Judgment%20(07.27.2022).pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guidance letter}}}\uc2  on Texas law issued by Paxton following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince September 2021, millions of people of reproductive capacity in Texas and beyond have been denied dignified treatment as equal human beings,\u8221\'20\'1D reads the complaint, arguing that the stories of the five women \u8220\'20\'1Crepresent only the tip of the iceberg\u8221\'20\'1D. Before Roe was overturned, Texas passed a six-week ban in 2021 that up until that point was the strictest in the country.\par \par \uc2 None of the women are suing the doctors who treated them, nor are they trying to overturn the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Texas. The suit calls on Texas officials to make clear the scope of the exceptions in the state\u8217\'20\'19s various bans that allow for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPregnant people in Texas and throughout the country have suffered unnecessary physical and emotional pain and harm, including loss of their fertility \u8230\'20\'26 These people are not hypothetical. They are not unknown. They are real people with families, many with children already,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit states.\par \par \uc2 It continues: \u8220\'20\'1CThe bans are exposing pregnant people to risks of death, injury and illness, including loss of fertility \u8211\'20\'13 making it less likely that every family who wants to bring children into the world will be able to do so and survive the experience. Medical professionals are now telling their patients that if they want to become pregnant, they should leave Texas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230307ej37003h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
