\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Diabolical liberty: after-school Satanists club threatens to sue district over ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 531 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par 18:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Club in Pennsylvania was initially approved to use facilities, but was rescinded after pushback from community members\par \par \uc2 An after-school Satanists club in Pennsylvania is threatening to raise hell after local district leaders denied them the ability to convene on their school grounds.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU)\uc2 , together with its Pennsylvania chapter, sent a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/2023-2-3-ACLU-Letter-to-SVSD-.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  to the Saucon Valley school district demanding that they allow the After School Satan Club, or ASSC, access to school facilities in accordance with the US constitution\u8217\'20\'19s first amendment right to practice religion freely.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  alleges that the Satanist club was initially approved to use district facilities, but that approval was rescinded after district officials received pushback from community members. The club\u8217\'20\'19s requested meeting dates were subsequently denied.\par \par \uc2 The After School Satan Club says it is a secular organization and its members do not actually believe in or worship the devil. According to their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thesatanictemple.com/pages/after-school-satan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 website}}}\uc2 , the club \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not believe in introducing religion into public schools and will only open a club if other religious groups are operating on campus\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://6abc.com/after-school-satan-club-satanic-group-saucon-valley-district-lehigh-news/12862066/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 By contrast}}}\uc2 , the Good News Club, an organization sponsored by a local evangelical church devoted to spreading the word about the Bible, is allowed to host meetings on public school property.\par \par \uc2 In the letter addressed to the district, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe district has intentionally opened up its facilities for general community use and, in so doing, may not limit access to this forum based on the content of our clients\u8217\'20\'19 speech, their religious identity, or their viewpoint \u8211\'20\'13 even if some may find their beliefs \u8216\'20\'18controversial or divisive\u8217\'20\'19. Nor may the district restrict our clients\u8217\'20\'19 access to this forum based on others\u8217\'20\'19 animus toward our clients\u8217\'20\'19 religion, or based on the anticipated or actual reactions to the content or viewpoint of our clients\u8217\'20\'19 speech.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  is threatening the school district with a lawsuit on behalf of the club and the Satanic Temple if the alleged discrimination continues.\par \par \uc2 Sara Rose, deputy legal director for the ACLU of Pennsylvania, told the Guardian: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unfortunate that the school district is doubling down on its unconstitutional and discriminatory action against the club and The Satanic Temple. We are consulting with our clients as they carefully consider their next steps.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The incident represents the larger debate about religious freedom in the US. Religion has served as a battleground for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, pitting those who believe in patient autonomy and a person\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose whether or not they want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against religious anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a sin.\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time self-described Satanists in America have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/04/friend-of-satan-how-lucien-greaves-and-his-satanic-temple-are-fighting-the-religious-right"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 waged war}}}\uc2  against the religious far-right. The Satanic Temple group, based in Massachusetts, has a long history of advocating on issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, prayer in classrooms and the distribution of Bibles in schools.\par \par \uc2 Satanic Temple members do not believe in Satan in a literal sense, but see Lucifer as a symbol of rebellion and opposition to authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 The Saucon Valley school district did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230308ej38002uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
