\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 South Carolina woman arrested for allegedly using pills to end pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2023\par 20:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Incident took place in 2021, but warrant was issued in 2022 and she was arrested in February, police confirm\par \par \uc2 A woman in South Carolina has been arrested and charged for allegedly trying to induce her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  using medication.\par \par \uc2 The woman was reported by staff at a hospital in Greenville, South Carolina, after visiting a hospital in October 2021 with labor pains. Ultimately, she delivered a stillborn fetus at about 25 weeks and four days of pregnancy. In an incident report, staff claimed the woman had admitted to having taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in an attempt to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The incident took place in 2021, before the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overturned in June 2022. But a warrant was subsequently issued for the woman\u8217\'20\'19s arrest in 2022, and she was arrested in February 2023, Sgt Jonathan Bragg, of the Greenville police department confirmed.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina is one of just three states, alongside Nevada and Oklahoma, that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t44c041.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 explicitly criminalizes self-managed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . The state was also the first to bring charges on behalf of a fetus successfully against a pregnant person. In 1997, Cornelia Whitner was found guilty for criminal child neglect, after taking crack cocaine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://caselaw.findlaw.com/sc-supreme-court/1238430.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 during the third trimester of her pregnancy}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s one of the states that really leads the country in pregnancy-related criminalization,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dana Sussman, of the non-profit Pregnancy Justice.\par \par \uc2 Broadly speaking, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are written to exempt pregnant people from prosecution for seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care , though there are signs this is beginning to change, with some lawmakers recently introducing bills to bring charges against people who seek abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe rights and humanity of pregnant people have been at risk for some time, even before Dobbs \u8211\'20\'13 and now it\u8217\'20\'19s just even more critical for us to be vigilant in defending the rights of pregnant people in a country that has decided that fertilized eggs, embryos and fetuses deserve more protection than the pregnant people who have full constitutional rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sussman. \u8220\'20\'1CThese types of [arrests], while quite rare, create a culture of fear and uncertainty that can have drastic ripple effects.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds have also raised concern over the incident.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe support and endorse folks accessing [safe] \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care if that feels right for them. The salient point for us for this case is: was this person choosing to allegedly self-manage because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t feel like they had access to different kinds of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care?\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson for the Carolina \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CCriminalising pregnancy outcomes generally is very, very dangerous for everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overturned by the supreme court in 2022, a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban quickly came into effect in South Carolina, but was blocked by a court in August. Earlier this year, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/05/south-carolina-abortion-ban-state-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state supreme court found}}}\uc2  the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban unconstitutional, and lawmakers are currently in talks to pass a new one. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently remains legal in the state up to 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Greenville police department confirmed that the woman was arrested and subsequently released the next day after making $2,500 bail. She awaits trial.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230303ej33003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
