\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Louisiana anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  group calls on doctors to stop denying care exempted by ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Poet Wolfe in Baton Rouge, Louisiana \par \uc2 1,200 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2023\par 14:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Group speaks out after hospitals refused to offer treatment for a woman who had a near deadly miscarriage citing ambiguous law\par \par \uc2 An influential group in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/louisiana"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana}}}\uc2  that has long opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is calling out medical providers and their legal advisers who \u8211\'20\'13 for an apparent fear of liability \u8211\'20\'13 have cited the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on most abortions to deny treatments that remain legal.\par \par \uc2 The group spoke out after hospitals in the state\u8217\'20\'19s capital, Baton Rouge, refused to provide treatments for a woman who had a near deadly miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 The treatments which Kaitlyn Joshua needed were similar to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and her doctors feared being prosecuted, citing purported ambiguities in the ban on terminating most pregnancies which took effect in Louisiana after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  last year overturned the nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights granted by Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Even though Louisiana has some of the tightest restrictions against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, Joshua was legally entitled to the care she sought under an exception to the ban which involves miscarriages, Sarah Zagorski of Louisiana Right for Life said.\par \par \uc2 Zagorski, whose organization has been involved in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation since 1970, said it is clear under Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that it is legal to provide and receive miscarriage treatments, even if they closely resemble some abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was just a gross misunderstanding of the law from the practitioners handling the case, unfortunately,\u8221\'20\'1D Zagorski said.\par \par \uc2 In a recent interview with the Guardian, Zagorski said the public in general urgently needs more education on the exceptions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in a state which has the highest maternal mortality rate in the US. While she stopped short of saying what her organization might be able to contribute that effort, she did say it was imperative for medical providers and their legal teams to take it upon themselves to study and comprehend the exceptions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Louisiana, especially in light of a case like the one centering on Joshua.\par \par \uc2 Joshua for her part has retained an attorney, though neither she nor the lawyer would comment to the Guardian on what actions they are possibly contemplating against any providers who turned Joshua away.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe law itself is very specific about this,\u8221\'20\'1D Zagorski added. \u8220\'20\'1CThis should not have been how this happened.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban states, in part: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shall not mean any one or more of the following acts, if performed by a physician: \u8230\'20\'26 the removal of a dead unborn child or the inducement or delivery of the uterine contents in case of a positive diagnosis, certified in writing in the woman\u8217\'20\'19s medical record along with the results of an obstetric ultrasound test, that the pregnancy has ended or is in the unavoidable and untreatable process of ending due to spontaneous miscarriage, also known in medical terminology as spontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , missed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , inevitable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , incomplete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or septic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To the author of the ban, the Louisiana state senator Katrina Jackson, the language makes it clear that miscarriage treatment is distinct from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Though she did not speak with the Guardian, she has previously released a statement to National Public Radio and its local New Orleans affiliate, WWNO, saying that nothing in the law bans women from receiving miscarriage treatments.\par \par \uc2 But Jackson has not indicated whether she may try to at all clarify the legislation she authored. She first faced calls to do at least that after Louisiana woman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/14/louisiana-woman-skull-less-fetus-new-york-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nancy Davis}}}\uc2 , who was carrying a skull-less fetus that would die within a short time of birth, was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state and had to travel to New York to terminate the pregnancy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access advocates have similarly rallied around Joshua.\par \par \uc2 At six weeks pregnant with her second child, Joshua called a physician group in Baton Rouge \u8211\'20\'13 her state\u8217\'20\'19s capital \u8211\'20\'13 to schedule her first prenatal appointment, but the clinic denied her an appointment. The group said it was no longer providing prenatal care for women under 12 weeks of pregnancy because it thought it was too risky in light of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that took effect in Louisiana after last year\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade to be ambiguous.\par \par \uc2 Miscarriages most frequently occur during the first trimester of pregnancy, and they require the same medical procedures as abortions, Joshua \u8211\'20\'13 who declined to speak to the Guardian \u8211\'20\'13 was told. Joshua told WWNO that the clinic did not want to face possibly being investigated if their miscarriage care was interpreted as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a Black woman, Joshua told WWNO that she was aware of maternal-related deaths in her state. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ldh.la.gov/news/4722"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2018 report by the Louisiana Department of Health}}}\uc2  found that Black women are four times more likely than their white counterparts to die during childbirth, so she decided to schedule her next appointment with a Black obstetrician.\par \par \uc2 Yet before her appointment, Joshua bled heavily and felt severe pain between her 10th and 11th week of pregnancy. She went to Woman\u8217\'20\'19s hospital in Baton Rouge for immediate care and received an ultrasound that showed her fetus had a faint heartbeat and had stopped growing three or four weeks earlier.\par \par \uc2 Joshua\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy hormones, meanwhile, were abnormally low. Nonetheless, the hospital would not confirm that she was having a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 By the next evening, Joshua ended up at Baton Rouge general hospital after losing a large amount of blood and tissue. A female doctor told Joshua that there appeared to be a cyst in her ultrasound and questioned if she was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Joshua told WWNO that the doctor recommended waiting at home for the miscarriage to pass, if this was in fact a spontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the doctor refused to give her treatments that would lessen the pain and quicken the miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe stated that they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to put \u8230\'20\'26 \u8216\'20\'18spontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 [anywhere] because that would then flag an investigation on them,\u8221\'20\'1D Joshua told WWNO.\par \par \uc2 Zagorski says it\u8217\'20\'19s natural for things to be confusing for providers and patients after last year\u8217\'20\'19s landmark supreme court decision. Nonetheless, Joshua\u8217\'20\'19s ordeal was separate and apart and clearly fit the built-in exceptions, she said.\par \par \uc2 For the record, Zagorski said neither her group nor the ban support aborting a child with life-threatening medical conditions, as seen in the Nancy Davis case. \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe that even in dire severe cases like that, where the baby is likely to not live long, that it is still a human life and there are ways that a woman can deliver naturally and have hospice care for that baby,\u8221\'20\'1D Zagorski said.\par \par \uc2 Despite that stance, Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s state health department issued an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.doa.la.gov/media/33xpvsds/2302emr013.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emergency rule}}}\uc2  late last month that allows women to terminate pregnancies if their unborn child suffers from one or more of 25 listed medical conditions, including acrania. The medical diagnoses remain an exception to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law for at most 180 days.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access advocates would prefer Louisiana and other similarly situated states to do away with their bans altogether. But the legislatures of Louisiana and those other states are controlled by conservatives who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230226ej2q002gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
