\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I cried with her\u8217\'20\'19: the diary of a doctor navigating a total  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2023\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Tennessee passed one of the US\u8217\'20\'19s strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, Dr Leilah Zahedi-Spung was unable to care for her patients\par \par \uc2 Dr Leilah Zahedi-Spung always knew providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Tennessee was going to be hard, but she probably never could have imagined how hard. On 25 August 2022, 18 months after Zahedi-Spung landed a dream job as a maternal fetal medicine specialist, the state enacted one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country, one that does not even make explicit exceptions to save the life of the pregnant person.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s ban makes performing or attempting to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a Class C felony \u8211\'20\'13 meaning Zahedi-Spung could have faced a 15-year-prison sentence for providing life-saving care. So, in January 2023, she decided to leave for work in Colorado, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal in all stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 At the end of 2022, she started writing a diary for the Guardian, detailing her last days in a busy doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office where she sometimes saw more than 40 patients a day, many begging her for help she could not give. The diary has been supplemented with interviews, to try and capture what life is like for a doctor whose work is severely restricted due to a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 30 November 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18utter and complete chaos\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 This patient knew around 15 weeks that her pregnancy was going to be unviable, but in the confusion that followed the overturning of Roe v Wade last June, she struggled to make a decision about whether to leave the state for care or to wait it out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was such utter and complete chaos that she just couldn\u8217\'20\'19t figure out what to do,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. As her doctor, Zahedi-Spung worried the longer the pregnancy went on, the harder it would be for her patient to let go. Eventually, they made plans to go ahead with the birth in Tennessee, through cesarian section.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung and I discuss what happened with that patient in a phone call. \u8220\'20\'1CShe carried to term and got delivered on Friday [9 December], and her baby died within a couple of minutes after delivery. But she got some time with her and that\u8217\'20\'19s what she wanted,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 Another patient came to see Zahedi-Spung alone on 30 November, having carried a pregnancy she had long warned was at high risk of being unsuccessful. The patient had been hopeful, although she was aware of a fetal anomaly. At 36 weeks, Zahedi-Spung diagnosed a stillbirth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had to tell her that her baby\u8217\'20\'19s dead. I mean, it\u8217\'20\'19s just it\u8217\'20\'19s awful every time. But it\u8217\'20\'19s really awful when they\u8217\'20\'19re alone,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung, reflecting on the case.\par \par \uc2 Later on 30 November \u8211\'20\'13 no heartbeat detected\par \par \uc2 Under these circumstances, Zahedi-Spung was allowed to intervene with a dilation and evacuation procedure, without having to send this patient out of state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have a heartbeat, it\u8217\'20\'19s not considered an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 1 December 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 patient transferred out\par \par \uc2 of\par \par \uc2 state\par \par \uc2 With this patient, Zahedi-Spung saw someone whose pregnancy had implanted in the scar of her uterus, a life threatening condition that can lead to a catastrophic uterine rupture. In other cases, it may result in the placenta eating through the wall of the uterus, the bladder and sometimes the bowel. Still, the Tennessee ban does not permit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for such cases.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung sent the couple to Georgia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are stringent, but include exceptions for maternal life and ectopic pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI sent her to one of my mentors, so I knew she was going to get exactly the care she needed,\u8221\'20\'1D Zahedi-Spung says.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 6 December 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18I feel like a shell of myself\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Up until this point, everything had been developing normally for the patient Zahedi-Spung saw on 8 December, as is often the case: most anomalies are not picked up before 16 weeks. The patient was happy and excited about their pregnancy. When they heard the pregnancy was nonviable, they were devastated.\par \par \uc2 Their options were limited. In terms of states nearby they could travel to, many were blocked off: Georgia had a six-week ban in operation, and nearby Alabama, Arkansas and Mississippi all had total bans. North Carolina was the closest state the couple would be able to receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care at this stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung worried about sending them to North Carolina. \u8220\'20\'1C[I was sending them] to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic \u8230\'20\'26 which comes with its own burden of having to walk past protesters who are like, \u8216\'20\'18you\u8217\'20\'19re gonna kill your baby!\u8217\'20\'19 and it\u8217\'20\'19s just awful for them. It\u8217\'20\'19s awful for anybody, but it\u8217\'20\'19s really awful for my patients who have lethal anomalies when it was a really wanted pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 While writing this diary, Zahedi-Spung was in the process of moving for a new job in Colorado. We caught up by phone in the middle of the day on a Friday, after she had just put her child down for nap time. She was quite sleepy herself, noticeable from her tone \u8211\'20\'13 she sounded relaxed but depleted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m exhausted by everything. I feel a little bit like a shell of myself at times,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. To cope with the stress, she says: \u8220\'20\'1CI tend to retreat inward. I describe myself as having a box [in my mind] that I put all this bad stuff in. And I shut it in very tight and I stick it on a shelf.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 20 December 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18Thankfully she didn\u8217\'20\'19t bleed to death\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 This patient came in around 16 weeks pregnant, having felt a gush of fluid from what\u8217\'20\'19s called a preterm premature rupture of membranes. Her water had broken early. She was with her partner and her mom, having had a totally normal pregnancy up until that point. She was in an incredible amount of pain, contracting every five minutes and crying. The amount of pain indicated she may go into labor soon, too early for the pregnancy to be viable. A heartbeat was still detectable on the ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI figured she\u8217\'20\'19d deliver at some point. But I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know when \u8211\'20\'13 and she was certainly not able to be discharged home given the amount of pain she was in. She was not infected; she certainly wasn\u8217\'20\'19t bleeding enough for me to justify doing anything. So we just had to wait, and I made her as comfortable as I could \u8211\'20\'13 we gave her an epidural,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully she didn\u8217\'20\'19t get sick and she didn\u8217\'20\'19t bleed to death. But why do I have to wait for that to take care of people?\par \par \uc2 If there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t a total ban in place, Zahedi-Spung would have induced labor, or taken the patient to the operating room to perform a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the rules said she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 The patient delivered around 12 hours later, after Zahedi-Spung had gone home.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTechnically we didn\u8217\'20\'19t break any standards of care, but the evidence-based guidelines are that if someone\u8217\'20\'19s water breaks prior to 22 weeks, you end the pregnancy. So I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t doing all that I could have done to protect the patient. Thankfully, she didn\u8217\'20\'19t get sick and she didn\u8217\'20\'19t bleed to death. But why do I have to wait for [that] to take care of people?\u8221\'20\'1D asks Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 The case made Zahedi-Spung angry. \u8220\'20\'1CI have the ability to provide life-saving care. And I\u8217\'20\'19m being told I\u8217\'20\'19m not allowed to do that because it offends people. It\u8217\'20\'19s so ridiculous. None of these lawmakers even understand the grief that these patients are suffering,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 22-26 December 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 An \u8216\'20\'18unexpected and terrible\u8217\'20\'19 discovery\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung saw this patient and her partner over four days around Christmas. The couple had just learned about a heart defect from their family doctor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were a very knowledgeable couple. This was an unexpected pregnancy, but a wanted one. And then this very unexpected and terrible finding of a heart defect showed up,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. Babies with this heart defect have a very small chance of surviving the first year of their life. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were very thoughtful, as all of my families are, about what that would mean for their life and for their kiddo\u8217\'20\'19s life,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. They decided to end the pregnancy out of state.\par \par \uc2 The heartbeat bears are a memento Zahedi-Spung offers to patients when a pregnancy won\u8217\'20\'19t be successful. A company sends a stuffed teddy and a recording device, and Zahedi-Spung records the heartbeat and puts the device inside bear. The families can squeeze it and hear the fetal heartbeat. \u8220\'20\'1CIt can be very important to families,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. She learned about the practice during her residency training.\par \par \uc2 2 January 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 the last day\par \par \uc2 This was Zahedi-Spung\u8217\'20\'19s last day. She was getting ready to leave at the end of her shift to go to the airport with her family when she was told about an emergency patient who was seizing and pregnant, around 23 or 24 weeks. The patient was unable to breathe on her own and had to be intubated. Neither the patient, nor her partner or family, seemed to know she was pregnant. They were shocked when Zahedi-Spung informed them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was really difficult because it meant we couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have a conversation about what it [would mean for the patient\u8217\'20\'19s health] to be delivered at this time, what it means for the baby, or anything like that,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung delivered the baby alive, but is unsure whether it survived after she left. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was very much on the edge,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd she just woke up and found out about a baby she hadn\u8217\'20\'19t known about when she came in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf there was no ban, I would have given her the option to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She was so sick, the kid was going to be sick. She was going to be impacted for the rest of her life by the surgery I had to give her. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know if she would have said yes to that, but I would have liked to have given her the option. Her hands were tied,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung\u8217\'20\'19s last day at work in Tennessee was 1 January. To mark the occasion, a colleague took her out for dinner at a farm-to-table restaurant in Northshore, Chattanooga. They split the chicken, and drank martinis. \u8220\'20\'1CShe was very sad that I was leaving. I was too. It\u8217\'20\'19s been bittersweet,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zahedi-Spung. \u8220\'20\'1CI never wanted to leave. I loved my job. It had just become impossible for me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her new job started on 17 January. Before she began, she still felt conflicted over starting out again, in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve only ever known how to take care of patients in a place where it\u8217\'20\'19s hard,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Being one of the only doctors that provided the care she did in Tennessee created a unique relationship. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of the patients I have taken care of over the last year and a half have my cell phone number. And that creates a different kind of bond with people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As she left Tennessee, she felt a nagging guilt.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPart of me felt like I was selling out by coming here,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s part of medicine. They teach us that we\u8217\'20\'19re not worthy, unless it is so hard. But I shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to sacrifice so much of myself for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230222ej2m001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
