\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Florida couple unable to get  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  will see baby die after delivery\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2023\par 17:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Doctors\u8217\'20\'19 interpretation of state law prevents procedure, family tells \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , despite baby\u8217\'20\'19s fatal illness\par \par \uc2 In a few weeks, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/florida"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}\uc2  couple will have to bid farewell to their child shortly after the baby is delivered, a gut-wrenching reality created by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s elimination of nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights last year.\par \par \uc2 Because of a new Florida law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks except under certain circumstances, Deborah Dorbert has become one of many women having difficulty accessing necessary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  procedures after the supreme court overturned the rights granted by the landmark 1973 Roe v Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2023/02/18/florida-abortion-ban-unviable-pregnancy-potter-syndrome/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  chronicles how Dorbert and her husband, Lee Dorbert, are expecting their second child and have been told by doctors that the baby has been diagnosed with a fatal fetal abnormality known as Potter syndrome. But, they have said, the doctors could not perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of their interpretation of a Florida law that took effect after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Potter syndrome is a rare condition related to a fetus\u8217\'20\'19s development in the uterus. The syndrome is a result of abnormal kidney growth and function, which affects how much amniotic fluid surrounds the fetus during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 It has been deemed a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2023/02/18/florida-abortion-ban-unviable-pregnancy-potter-syndrome/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doubly lethal diagnosis}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D because babies with malfunctioning kidneys can\u8217\'20\'19t remove deadly toxins from their bodies and can in turn experience renal failure. Additionally, the absence of amniotic fluid in a womb causes a baby to be born without the ability to breathe.\par \par \uc2 According to Florida\u8217\'20\'19s Reducing Fetal and Infant Mortality {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.flsenate.gov/Committees/billsummaries/2022/html/2725#:~:text=The%20bill%20prohibits%20a%20physician,pregnant%20woman's%20last%20menstrual%20period."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law}}}\uc2 , which was implemented last July, abortions are prohibited after 15 weeks of gestation, with a few exceptions, including one that would allow for a later \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cif two physicians certify in writing that the fetus has a fatal fetal abnormality and has not reached viability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last November, when the couple\u8217\'20\'19s baby was diagnosed with the syndrome, a maternal fetal medicine specialist told the Dorberts that some parents choose to continue to full term while others opt to terminate the pregnancy through surgery or preterm labor.\par \par \uc2 The doctor added that he would consult with health system administrators regarding the new law, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports. The Dorberts eventually decided that they would like to terminate the pregnancy as early as they could because babies with the syndrome often die before they are born or end up suffocating within minutes or hours after their delivery.\par \par \uc2 Deborah Dorbert told the outlet that she recalled the specialist saying that the termination might be possible \u8211\'20\'13 but not until between 28 and 32 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Then, after the specialist consulted with health system administrators regarding the new law, the couple was told that they would have to wait to terminate the pregnancy until the 37th week of gestation \u8211\'20\'13 or near full term.\par \par \uc2 According to a text message Deborah Dorbert received from the coordinator at a maternal fetal medicine office that she visits often, the specialist made his determination after having legal administrators \u8220\'20\'1Clook at the new law and the way it\u8217\'20\'19s written\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s horribly written,\u8221\'20\'1D the message added.\par \par \uc2 Despite the specialist telling the couple that other states had fewer restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the Dorberts told the Post that they were overwhelmed by travel costs and had only left their state a few times.\par \par \uc2 The couple, who have not learned the baby\u8217\'20\'19s sex because its legs were crossed or the umbilical cord was in the way during each scan, eventually opted to provide palliative care to their child after the baby is born.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s been very important to us, understanding that we do have that control back at least in some of these decisions,\u8221\'20\'1D Lee Dorbert told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the new law \u8211\'20\'13 which punishes physicians who violate it with penalties including license revocation, hefty fines and five years or more of jail time \u8211\'20\'13 has left the couple angry and frustrated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt makes me angry, for politicians to decide what\u8217\'20\'19s best for my health,\u8221\'20\'1D Deborah Dorbert told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe would do anything to have this baby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have never really understood,\u8221\'20\'1D Lee Dorbert said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CWe were told there was an exception \u8230\'20\'26 Obviously, [it\u8217\'20\'19s] not enough of an exception in some cases.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite the pain that the Dorberts and couples in similar situations are experiencing, the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , has maintained a staunch anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, DeSantis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.clickorlando.com/news/politics/2023/02/01/we-will-sign-florida-gov-desantis-champions-potential-6-week-abortion-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  that he would sign a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if one passed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re for pro-life,\u8221\'20\'1D DeSantis said. \u8220\'20\'1CI urge the legislature to work, produce good stuff, and we will sign. That\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19ve always wanted to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230218ej2i0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
