\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Virginia governor blocks bill banning police from seeking menstrual histories\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe \par \uc2 581 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2023\par 16:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Glenn Youngkin essentially kills bill passed in Democratic-led state senate to ban search warrants for menstrual data on tracking apps\par \par \uc2 The Republican governor of Virginia, Glenn Youngkin, appears to have thwarted an attempt to stop law enforcement obtaining menstrual histories of women in the state.\par \par \uc2 A bill passed in the Democratic-led state senate, and supported by half the chamber\u8217\'20\'19s Republicans, would have banned search warrants for menstrual data stored in tracking apps on mobile phones or other electronic devices.\par \par \uc2 Advocates feared private health information could be used in prosecutions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law violations, after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court ruling}}}\uc2  last summer overturned federal protections for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But Youngkin, who has pushed for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wric.com/news/politics/capitol-connection/republican-lawmakers-introduce-gov-youngkins-15-week-abortion-ban-proposal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  to mirror {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/dec/13/abortion-restrictions-us-state-legislatures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 similar measures}}}\uc2  in several Republican-controlled states, essentially killed the bill through a procedural move in a subcommittee of the Republican-controlled House.\par \par \uc2 Citing unspecified future threats to the ability of law enforcement to investigate crime, Maggie Cleary, Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s deputy secretary of public safety, told the courts of justice subcommittee it was not the legislature\u8217\'20\'19s responsibility to restrict the scope of search warrants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the administration understands the importance of individuals\u8217\'20\'19 privacy \u8230\'20\'26 this bill would be the very first of its kind that I\u8217\'20\'19m aware of, in Virginia or anywhere, that would set a limit on what search warrants can do,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/02/14/youngkin-menstrual-data-abortion-virginia/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Washington Post}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCurrently any health information or any app information is available via search warrant. And we believe that should continue to be the case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The panel voted on party line to table the bill, meaning it is unlikely to resurface during the current legislative session.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates contend that with Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to push a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with limited exceptions, failing to advance in either legislative chamber, the governor is looking for other avenues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Youngkin administration\u8217\'20\'19s opposition to this commonsense privacy protection measure shows his real intentions, to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and criminalise patients and medical providers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tarina Keene, executive director of Repro Rising Virginia, in a statement provided to the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has insisted that any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions would target doctors, not women who have the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The administration has also attempted to portray a united front among Republicans for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, arguing it is a consensus issue. But the defection of the nine senate Republicans over the menstrual data bill follows one of their number, Siobhan Dunnavant, speaking out last month against Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week proposal.\par \par \uc2 Dunnavant, an ob-gyn doctor, condemned the bill as \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.virginiamercury.com/2023/01/20/republican-senator-breaks-with-gop-to-oppose-15-week-abortion-bill-in-virginia/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Virginia Mercury}}}\uc2 , and said she could not support it unless it contained an exception for severe fetal abnormalities to 24 weeks. Under current Virginia law, the procedure is legal for all women until the 27th week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The wrangle over menstrual data tracking has parallels with a controversy in Florida, in which high school athletics officials last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2023/feb/09/florida-cuts-humiliating-questions-about-school-athletes-menstrual-histories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backed away from a \u8220\'20\'1Chumiliating\u8221\'20\'1D proposal}}}\uc2  requiring girls who wanted to play sports to answer questions about menstruation on medical forms.\par \par \uc2 Critics said the requirement aligned with a push by the Republican governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/28/dont-say-gay-bill-florida-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curtail transgender rights}}}\uc2 , an allegation denied by high school officials.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article\u8217\'20\'19s headline was amended on 16 February 2023. An earlier version did not correctly describe the purpose of the menstrual data bill or Glenn Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s position on it.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230216ej2g000ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
