\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Turkey and Syria earthquake death toll \u8216\'20\'18could pass 20,000\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Mattha Busby \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2023\par 11:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  official warns that number killed could quadruple. Plus, efforts to save the pink river dolphin\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of powerful aftershocks continued to jolt southern Turkey and northern Syria on Tuesday, a day after an earthquake struck the region killing more than 5,000 people and destroying thousands of buildings, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/07/turkey-syria-earthquake-conditions-rescue-efforts-death-toll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 difficult conditions and freezing temperatures}}}\uc2  hampered rescue efforts.\par \par \uc2 As the scale of the devastation from the 7.8-magnitude tremor \u8211\'20\'13 which was followed by a second, 7.5-magnitude quake later in the day \u8211\'20\'13 continued to unfold, the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  warned the number of fatalities could exceed 20,000.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s vice-president, \uc2 Fuat Oktay\uc2 , said 3,419 people had been killed, with a further 20,534 injured. The number of confirmed deaths on the Syrian side of the border rose to 1,602, bringing the death toll in both countries to 5,021. Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s disaster management agency said it had 11,342 reports of collapsed buildings, of which 5,775 had been confirmed.\par \par \uc2 People in remote towns in southern Turkey described how relief efforts were stretched to breaking point, with destruction over a border region spanning almost 650 miles.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There is a family I know under the rubble\u8217\'20\'19. Amid freezing rain and an unprecedented disaster, survivors dig in the rubble and listen for screams in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/07/turkey-syria-earthquake-survivors-join-search-missing-people"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hope of reaching trapped loved ones}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Earthquakes add\par \par \uc2 to suffering in war-torn Syria. The 7.8-magnitude quake that struck the region in the early hours of Monday, and the strong second shock hours later, is adding to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/06/there-is-nothing-left-earthquake-adds-to-suffering-in-war-torn-syria"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already intolerable suffering}}}\uc2  for people who mostly survive thanks to aid.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  targets low-income US women with ads for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers, study shows\par \par \uc2 Low-income women in some cities are more likely than their wealthier counterparts to be targeted by \uc2 Google\uc2  ads promoting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers when they search for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/07/google-targets-low-income-women-anti-abortion-pregnancy-center-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 researchers at the Tech Transparency Project have found.}}}\par \par \uc2 The research builds on previous findings detailing how \uc2 Google\uc2  directs users searching for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis centers\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 organizations that have been known to pose as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in an attempt to steer women away from accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 For decades, misleading consumers has often been a key part of pregnancy centers\u8217\'20\'19 business model, researchers and advocacy groups found. While pregnancy crisis centers offer pregnant women resources such as diapers and pregnancy testing, they have also been known to employ a number of shady tactics to convince women seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Lower-income women suffer most. This is since they are least likely to be able to travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care because traveling can cost thousands of dollars in lost work, transportation, babysitting and accommodation fees.\par \par \uc2 Indigenous activist Leonard Peltier in plea for clemency after 47 years in jail\par \par \uc2 Leonard Peltier, the Indigenous rights activist held for almost five decades in maximum security for crimes he has always denied, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/06/leonard-peltier-interview-prison-48-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made a plea for clemency}}}\uc2  so that he can wander freely and hug his grandchildren for the first time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I was free I would build me a home on my tribal land, help build the economy of our nations and give a home to our homeless children,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peltier, who was convicted in processes rife with violations including evidence falsification by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peltier, an enrolled member of the Turtle Mountain Chippewa tribe and of Lakota and Dakota descent, was convicted of murdering two \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents during a shootout on the Pine Ridge reservation in South Dakota in June 1975. He was a leader of the American Indian Movement (Aim), an Indigenous civil rights movement founded in Minneapolis that was infiltrated and repressed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Condemnation has fallen on deaf ears. \uc2 Amnesty International\uc2 , \uc2 UN\uc2  experts, Nelson Mandela, the Dalai Lama and the Rev Jesse Jackson are among those to have previously condemned his prolonged detention as arbitrary and politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rural communities, former Farc guerrillas\par \par \uc2 and endangered dolphins are unlikely allies in a near-forgotten corner of Colombia\u8217\'20\'19s Amazon basin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/feb/06/rainforest-colombia-farc-amazon-river-dolphins-peace-aoe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where tourism is providing opportunities}}}\uc2  for reconciliation as well as creating jobs and promoting conservation.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 BP\u8217\'20\'19s annual profits more than doubled to $28bn in 2022 as a sharp increase in gas prices linked to the Ukraine war boosted its earnings, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/07/bp-profits-windfall-tax-gas-prices-ukraine-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 adding fuel to calls}}}\uc2  for a toughened windfall tax as oil companies reap rewards from higher consumer costs.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The diplomatic row has escalated over the Chinese high-altitude balloon that flew across the US before being shot down, as the first wreckage was salvaged off the Atlantic coast. Beijing accused the US of \u8220\'20\'1Coverreaction\u8221\'20\'1D and the \u8220\'20\'1Cindiscriminate use of military force\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: \u8216\'20\'18Carnival of democracy\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 celebration returns to Rio after a two-year hiatus\par \par \uc2 In Rio de Janeiro, the saying goes that carnival begins right after the new year. So since last month exuberant Cariocas dressed in their sparkliest and skimpiest outfits have sambaed through the city\u8217\'20\'19s historic centre to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/06/rio-carnival-returns-brazil-pandemic-hiatus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sound of percussion and brass bands}}}\uc2 , lured to the streets as the blocos \u8211\'20\'13 musical troupes that lead the street parties \u8211\'20\'13 proclaim the unofficial launch of carnival.\par \par \uc2 The atmosphere is particularly feverish this year, after the coronavirus pandemic forced a two-year hiatus that took an economic and emotional toll on Rio and its carnival-starved people, writes Constance Malleret. Although out-of-season samba parades were held last year, the street celebrations were restricted to a smattering of rebellious blocos that defied a ban on the free gatherings. Now, the pre-Lenten revelry is finally back in all its glory.\par \par \uc2 Climate check:\par \par \uc2 leaked video footage of ocean pollution shines light on deep-sea mining\par \par \uc2 Video footage from a deep-sea mining test, showing sediment discharging into the ocean, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/feb/06/leaked-video-footage-of-ocean-pollution-shines-light-on-deep-sea-mining"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raised fresh questions}}}\uc2  about the largely untested nature of the industry. \uc2 The Metals Company\uc2 , a Canadian mining firm that is one of the leading industry players, spent time last year testing its underwater extraction vehicle in the Clarion-Clipperton Fracture Zone, a section of the Pacific Ocean between Mexico and Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 But a group of scientists hired by the company to monitor its operations, concerned by what they saw, posted a video of what they said was a flawed process that accidentally released sediment into the ocean.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: why the generation gap isn\u8217\'20\'19t as wide as you think\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re bombarded with stories of a \u8220\'20\'1Cwoke\u8221\'20\'1D generation obsessed with \u8220\'20\'1Csafe spaces\u8221\'20\'1D and fostering a \u8220\'20\'1Ccancel culture\u8221\'20\'1D. But this is a misdirection. It is true that younger people have a different perspective on shifting social norms \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/feb/06/the-big-idea-why-the-generation-gap-isnt-as-wide-as-you-think"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 but that has always been the case}}}\uc2 , writes Bobby Duffy.\par \par \uc2 Younger generations are just more comfortable with new cultural ideas because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t grow up with the older ones. In fact, in Duffy\u8217\'20\'19s analysis of long trends, it\u8217\'20\'19s pretty much a constant that the youngest generation will be twice as comfortable with the latest cultural norm than the oldest. The issues change, but the generational patterns are eerily similar.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230207ej27001pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
