\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Monterey Park shooting suspect kills himself after attack\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 1,830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Suspected gunman who killed at least 10 people at California dance studio found dead. Plus, lab-grown meat set for takeoff in the US\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The suspect in a deadly mass shooting at a southern California dance studio on Saturday night {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/monterey-park-shooting-suspect-dead"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died from a self-inflicted gunshot wound}}}\uc2  on Sunday morning. Ten people were killed and at least 10 others were taken to hospital after a gunman opened fire in a ballroom dance studio in a city close to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The mass shooting, one of California\u8217\'20\'19s worst in recent memory, happened hours after a lunar new year festival that attracted tens of thousands of revellers in Monterey Park, a majority-Asian American city. The motive for the attack is still under investigation, law enforcement officials said at a press briefing last night.\par \par \uc2 Officials have identified the shooter as Huu Can Tran, a 72-year-old man. After a manhunt, Huu was found dead in a white van that police had identified as a suspicious vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Authorities said that after opening fire at one dance studio, the shooter appeared to have targeted a second dance venue in nearby Alhambra, another majority-Asian American town, where he fled after two people were able to take his gun away from him.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Who were the victims? The coroner\u8217\'20\'19s office is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/monterey-park-shooting-what-we-know-so-far"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cstill in the process of identifying\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  the victims who were killed, the Los Angeles county sheriff, Robert Luna, said on Sunday. The ages of the victims appear to be in their 50s, 60s and \u8220\'20\'1Cmaybe even some beyond there\u8221\'20\'1D, Luna said.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Do we know anything about the gun used? Two weapons have been identified so far, including a handgun with a high capacity magazine. The weapon recovered from the Alhambra location where the shooter was disarmed was described as a semiautomatic assault pistol with a high-capacity magazine attached, Luna said. He said it initially appeared that weapon would be illegal under California law.\par \par \uc2 Germany criticised for failing to supply tanks to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Heavy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/jan/23/russia-ukraine-war-germany-expects-decision-soon-on-delivery-of-tanks-to-ukraine-zelenskiy-vows-to-fight-corruption-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 diplomatic pressure has been building on Berlin}}}\uc2  to send its tanks to Ukraine, or at least allow countries that bought them from Germany to re-export them. As the producer of the Leopard tanks, Berlin has a veto on their transfer.\par \par \uc2 At a special international summit on Friday at the US military base in Ramstein, south-west Germany, Berlin declined to take a decision on whether to give Leopard 2 tanks to Ukraine, leading to growing frustration from Kyiv and its allies.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Poland\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister, \uc2 Mateusz Morawiecki\uc2 , criticised Germany\u8217\'20\'19s failure to supply the hardware to Ukraine: \u8220\'20\'1CGermany\u8217\'20\'19s attitude is unacceptable. It has been almost a year since the war began. Innocent people are dying every day. Russian bombs are wreaking havoc in Ukrainian cities. Civilian targets are being attacked, women and children are being murdered.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He went on: \u8220\'20\'1CI try to weigh my words but I\u8217\'20\'19ll say it bluntly: Ukraine and Europe will win this war \u8211\'20\'13 with or without Germany.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It had been hoped in Kyiv and the US that Germany would allow Leopards owned by countries such as Poland and Finland to be re-exported. However, Germany\u8217\'20\'19s defence minister, Boris Pistorius, said despite heightened expectations, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe still cannot say when a decision will be taken, and what the decision will be, when it comes to the Leopard tank\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Why is Germany hesitating? There are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jan/22/the-guardian-view-on-germany-and-ukraine-a-fateful-hesitation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many reasons}}}\uc2 , each individually understandable but which cumulatively underestimate the larger reality of Russian aggression. The worries include American caution, German history, reluctance to be Europe\u8217\'20\'19s military leader, divisions in German public opinion, coalition government unity, a new defence minister and, above all, future relations with Russia.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What else is happening? Here\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/23/russia-ukraine-war-at-a-glance-what-we-know-on-day-334-of-the-invasion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 what we know on day 334}}}\uc2  of the invasion.\par \par \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s March draws thousands across US after Roe v Wade overturned\par \par \uc2 Thousands of protesters gathered across the US on Sunday to protest the end of the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 marching on the 50th anniversary of the Roe v Wade supreme court decision that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a constitutional right in 1973, but which was struck down last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/womens-march-2023-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes Poppy Noor}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At more than 200 Women\u8217\'20\'19s March events in 46 states, demonstrators condemned the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, which leaves it up to individual states to decide whether to protect, ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Since last year\u8217\'20\'19s startling decision from the conservative-dominated bench, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been banned or severely restricted in 14 states, after the ruling on 24 June.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFifty years after the anniversary of Roe v Wade, a radical rightwing movement hijacked our courts and eliminated federal protections for abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Carmona, the executive director of the Women\u8217\'20\'19s March organization, who orchestrated the nationwide events.\par \par \uc2 Carmona added: \u8220\'20\'1CBut as the fight turns to the states, they are going to learn that the overwhelming majority of Americans in all states support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 and women will fight to protect our rights and our lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Why was the main event for the protest in Madison, Wisconsin? Women\u8217\'20\'19s March organizers chose Wisconsin to highlight the state\u8217\'20\'19s forthcoming state supreme court election, which could be pivotal, and mobilize protesters and activists.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will name as his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/joe-biden-new-chief-of-staff-jeff-zients"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new chief of staff Jeff Zients}}}\uc2 , a former top official for coordinating the US response to the coronavirus pandemic, after it had emerged on Saturday that the US president\u8217\'20\'19s current chief, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , plans to step down, according to US media reports.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell has questioned the authenticity of a photograph that came to symbolise Virginia Giuffre\u8217\'20\'19s sexual abuse allegations against Prince Andrew, amid reports the Duke of York is considering overturning his settlement. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/ghislaine-maxwell-prince-andrew-photo-virginia-giuffre-epstein"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video interview from prison}}}\uc2 , Maxwell said: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe it\u8217\'20\'19s real for a second.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 More than a thousand mourners gathered in Graceland on a chilly, grey Sunday morning to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jan/22/lisa-marie-presley-memorial-service-graceland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pay their respects to singer Lisa Marie Presley}}}\uc2  at the Memphis, Tennessee, mansion she inherited from her father, the rock legend Elvis Presley. Lisa Marie Presley died on 12 January at the age of 54.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A senior aide to Japan\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister, \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 , has apologised for putting his hands in his pockets during an official trip to the US, admitting that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/23/japan-pm-aide-told-off-by-his-ashamed-mother-for-putting-hands-in-pockets-on-biden-trip"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his mother had scolded him}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Seiji Kihara\uc2 , 52, received an angry phone call from his mother, who told him she was \u8220\'20\'1Cashamed\u8221\'20\'1D and suggested he \u8220\'20\'1Csew up his pockets\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: 100,000 people took to streets in Israel over rightwing government\u8217\'20\'19s judicial changes\par \par \uc2 An estimated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/22/huge-crowds-protest-in-israel-over-rightwing-governments-judicial-reforms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 100,000 people took to the streets of Tel Aviv}}}\uc2  on Saturday night in what protesters described as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfight for Israel\u8217\'20\'19s destiny\u8221\'20\'1D over sweeping judicial changes proposed by the new far-right government. Israel\u8217\'20\'19s longtime prime minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , returned to office last month at the helm of a coalition of conservative and religious parties that make up the most right-wing government in the country\u8217\'20\'19s history. The new administration has accused Israel\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court of leftwing bias and overstepping its authority. The Tel Aviv protest, along with smaller demonstrations in Jerusalem, Haifa and Beersheba, were sparked by fears that the far-reaching proposals undermine democratic norms.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: \u8216\'20\'18My education was in a bar\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 Shania Twain on childhood, stardom, divorce and survival\par \par \uc2 Usually, when music tells you to cheer up, or love yourself, or feel empowered, it\u8217\'20\'19s shallow or simplistic. But Shania Twain comes at it with the deadly seriousness of lived adversity. Even in the 90s, behind her showgirl, pop-crossover persona, all midriff and hats, there was gritty resilience. Queen of Me is only her sixth studio album, which is surprising, considering the length of her career (if you count from when she started singing in bars as a child, it covers five decades) and her profile (she is the bestselling female artist in country music history). There is a nice line in last year\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Netflix\uc2  documentary Not Just a Girl, when her manager, Jon Landau, says she \u8220\'20\'1Cleft a lot of money on the table\u8221\'20\'1D. Here she discusses {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jan/23/shania-twain-childhood-stardom-divorce-survival"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deprivation, domestic violence and feeling triumphant}}}\uc2  after stage fright and surgery.\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 Or this: Lost photos from Warsaw\par \par \uc2 ghetto\par \par \uc2 uprising reveal horror of Jews\u8217\'20\'19 last stand\par \par \uc2 The photographs are blurry, composed hastily and taken surreptitiously, sometimes with heads or objects in the foreground obscuring part of the view, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/22/lost-photos-warsaw-uprising-reveal-horror-jews-last-stand"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes Shaun Walker}}}\uc2 . But Holocaust historians say the imperfect pictures, discovered last month in a Polish attic decades after their creator died, are nonetheless priceless. They are the only known photographs from inside the Warsaw ghetto uprising not to be taken by Germans. The photographs will form part of an exhibition devoted to the 80th anniversary of the uprising, to be held in April at Warsaw\u8217\'20\'19s POLIN museum of Jewish history.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: Oilwells guzzle precious California water. Next door, residents can\u8217\'20\'19t use the tap\par \par \uc2 In Kern county, California, oilwells sit in the middle of fields of grapevines and almond trees, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/22/california-oil-wells-refineries-water-use-drought-fuller-acres"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes Hilary Beaumont}}}\uc2 . The air is heavy with dust and the scent of petroleum. The energy fields here are some of the most productive in the US, generating billions of barrels of oil annually and more than two-thirds of the state\u8217\'20\'19s natural gas. And in a drought-stricken state, they\u8217\'20\'19re also some of the thirstiest, consuming vast quantities of fresh water to extract stubborn oil. But in the industry\u8217\'20\'19s shadow, nearby communities can\u8217\'20\'19t drink from the tap. One of those communities is Fuller Acres, a largely Latino town in Kern county where residents must drive to the nearest town to buy safe water.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: I tried lab-grown meat \u8211\'20\'13 food made from animals without killing them\par \par \uc2 It was a confronting moment for a vegetarian, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jan/23/lab-grown-meat-animals-climate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes Oliver Milman}}}\uc2 . First, a pork meatball and then slices of bacon, balanced in a sort of mini BLT, were served to eat by beaming, expectant hosts. The meat even came from a named pig, an affable-looking swine called Dawn. With some trepidation, I sliced into the meatball and ate it. I then took a nibble of the bacon. It was my first taste of meat in 11 years, a confounding experience made possible by the fact that Dawn, gamboling in a field in upstate New York, did not die for this meal. Instead a clump of her cells were grown in a lab to create \u8220\'20\'1Ccultivated meat\u8221\'20\'1D. Set for takeoff in the US, a \u8220\'20\'1Charmless\u8221\'20\'1D sample from one pig can produce millions of tons of \u8220\'20\'1Ccultivated meat\u8221\'20\'1D and is touted as far better for the climate.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230123ej1n001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
