\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans squabble over  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  as 2024 primaries loom\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino and David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par 07:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Potential presidential contenders walk tightrope in demonstrating hardline credentials without alienating moderates\par \par \uc2 They came in their thousands, wearing hats, waving flags and exulting in the death of American women\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But some who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/20/march-for-life-anti-abortion-roe-v-wade-fall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 marched in Washington on Friday}}}\uc2  were also thinking ahead: who will be their next champion in the White House?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t for President Trump, we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have a post-Roe America,\u8221\'20\'1D said Patricia Stephanoff, 66, from Michigan, wearing a pink \u8220\'20\'1CTrump 2024\u8221\'20\'1D hat. \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s the most pro-life president we\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had. He\u8217\'20\'19s the only president who has ever come to the march.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not everyone on the first {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://marchforlife.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March for Life}}}\uc2  since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s June 2022 decision to overturn Roe v Wade saw it the same way, however. Yvette Griego, from New Mexico, said she preferred Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former vice-president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , because he stood for his convictions and beliefs.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is emerging as one of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/20/us/politics/abortion-republicans-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first animating issues}}}\uc2  and key point of differentiation in the nascent Republican presidential primary for 2024, with Trump, the one officially declared candidate, and various likely rivals already jostling for position.\par \par \uc2 Each faces a tightrope as they must demonstrate their hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  credentials to the base voters that dominate a Republican primary, then manage or mitigate the subject in a way that does not alienate independents and moderates in a general election.\par \par \uc2 The awkwardness was spelled out in last year\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, less than five months after the demise of Roe v Wade allowed states to enact near or total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Numerous Republican candidates stressed their opposition to reproductive rights during the primaries, only to then scrub such language from their campaign websites when they faced Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Voters were not fooled and Republicans underperformed, losing a seat in the Senate and gaining {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/16/us-midterms-2022-republicans-win-control-house-representatives-congress-midterm-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only a 10-seat majority}}}\uc2  in the House of Representatives. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists such as Tudor Dixon in Michigan, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  in Nevada and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania crashed and burned.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/poll-finding/2022-midterm-election-kff-ap-votecast-analysis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 An analysis}}}\uc2  by the the \uc2 Henry J Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  found that nearly half of voters said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade had a major impact on which candidates they supported in this election, with almost two-thirds of those voting for Democratic House candidates.\par \par \uc2 No one is finding the issue more vexing than Trump himself. Seeking to shift blame for Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 poor showing, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/03/donald-trump-abortion-midterms-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he said this month}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIt was the \u8216\'20\'18\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue\u8217\'20\'19, poorly handled by many Republicans, especially those that firmly insisted on no exceptions, even in the case of rape, incest, or life of the mother, that lost large numbers of voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The observation drew criticism from Christian evangelical leaders who have so far been slow to endorse Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 bid, perhaps aware that others may outflank him on the right. So it was no surprise this week when Trump sought to shore up his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  credentials, reminding conservatives that he was the one who tilted the balance of the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Real America\u8217\'20\'19s Voice on Monday, he said: \u8220\'20\'1CNobody has ever done more for right to life than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I put three supreme court justices, who all voted, and they got something that they\u8217\'20\'19ve been fighting for 64 years, for many, many years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not for the first time, Trump seems to have few genuine ideological beliefs. In his days as a New York property developer and celebrity, he said he was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/trump-in-1999-i-am-very-pro-choice-480297539914"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-choice}}}\uc2 . But during his 2016 election, he declared \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/mar/30/donald-trump-women-abortions-punishment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 there has to be some form of punishment}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for women who have abortions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lincolnproject.us/bios/stuart-stevens/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stuart Stevens}}}\uc2 , a co-founder of the Lincoln Project, an anti-Trump group, said: \u8220\'20\'1CTrump has been all over the place on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He\u8217\'20\'19s had more positions than George Santos has names. He was adamantly pro-choice at one time before he ran for president.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pence, whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/18/mike-pence-trump-lite-politeness-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently published memoir}}}\uc2  is titled So Help Me God, is a more convincing zealot. He has endorsed a national 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban proposed last year by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina. His uncompromising stance could be a unique selling point in states such as Iowa in the Republican primaries \u8211\'20\'13 and a serious liability in the general election.\par \par \uc2 The former vice-president told the Daily Signal this week that he \u8220\'20\'1Cstrongly\u8221\'20\'1D disagrees with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments about the midterms, contending that candidates with a \u8220\'20\'1Cclear, unambiguous commitment to life\u8221\'20\'1D performed well.\par \par \uc2 Pence\u8217\'20\'19s non-profit organisation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://advancingamericanfreedom.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Advancing American Freedom}}}\uc2 , has proposed a law that would extend protections to embryos by declaring that life begins at the moment an egg is fertilised and a law that could open the way to banning Plan B emergency contraceptives and some forms of birth control.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Democratic National Committee{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democrats.org/news/new-pence-group-urges-congress-to-pass-extreme-abortion-bans/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in a press release}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CPence has made his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances the hallmark of his shadow campaign for the 2024 Republican primary \u8211\'20\'13 drawing his fellow GOP contenders to showcase their extremism as well as they each compete for the Maga base.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among them is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the governor of Florida currently leading Trump in some polls, who in April signed a bill banning abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 That did not go far enough for \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , the governor of South Dakota. Her spokesperson, Ian Fury, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalreview.com/corner/kristi-noem-spokesman-unloads-on-ron-desantis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the conservative National Review magazine}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CGovernor Noem was the only governor in America on national television defending the Dobbs decision [that overturned Roe v Wade]. Where was Governor DeSantis? Hiding behind a 15-week ban. Does he believe that 14-week-old babies don\u8217\'20\'19t have a right to live?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The surprise attack might reflect Noem\u8217\'20\'19s presidential ambitions \u8211\'20\'13 or an effort to catch the eye and curry favour with Trump as a possible pick for running mate. Meanwhile Glenn Youngkin, the governor of Virginia and another potential candidate, has thrown his weight behind a 15-week ban in the state with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , a former secretary of state, has promoted his work in the Trump administration reimplementing and expanding the \u8220\'20\'1CMexico City policy\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a ban on US foreign aid for overseas groups that make referrals for abortions or give patients information about the procedure. He tweeted last year: \u8220\'20\'1CNow, with Roe overturned, we will see which politicians supported the pro-life cause to win elections, and which actually believed it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Each is eager to impress anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups likely to carry huge sway in the Republican primary. On a call with reporters this week, Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sbaprolife.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Susan B Anthony List}}}\uc2 , said any Republican hopeful who shied away from pushing for new federal restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cdisqualified him or herself as a presidential candidate in our eyes and, having done so, has very little chance of winning the nomination\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dannenfelser said she and her team would meet potential nominees in the coming months. She held talks recently with DeSantis and was \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely satisfied\u8221\'20\'1D by his commitment to advancing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in the state. She said he had described the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks as a \u8220\'20\'1Cstart\u8221\'20\'1D but added that the governor did not know yet what his next steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be.\par \par \uc2 Above all, Dannenfelser warned candidates not to adopt the \u8220\'20\'1Costrich strategy\u8221\'20\'1D, which she described as a misguided \u8211\'20\'13 and electorally costly \u8211\'20\'13 effort by some Republicans to avoid discussing their position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2022, candidates who embraced the approach out of fear of alienating key voters fared poorly, she argued, compared with those who aggressively defended their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, such as DeSantis and the Florida senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would say if you could only give one lesson learned it would be the result of the ostrich strategy is disastrous for candidates,\u8221\'20\'1D she told reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CIf that\u8217\'20\'19s what happens in the coming federal elections, we will see the same result.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite last summer\u8217\'20\'19s triumph, the base remains hungry for more. Last week the Republican-controlled House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-politics-us-republican-party-united-states-senate-cb608d31f591fd06ec2fd753f122f6cb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed a resolution}}}\uc2  to condemn attacks on anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities, including crisis pregnancy centers, and a separate bill that would impose new penalties if a doctor refused to care for an infant born alive after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempt. Neither is expected to pass the Democratic-led Senate.\par \par \uc2 But last August voters in deep red Kansas delivered a warning to Republicans, decisively voting to continue to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protect \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  in the state constitution. Then came the letdown of the midterms. The issue could trouble Republicans again in House, Senate and governors\u8217\'20\'19 races next year.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://watson.brown.edu/people/faculty-fellows/schiller"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wendy Schiller}}}\uc2 , a political science professor at \uc2 Brown University\uc2  in Providence, Rhode Island, said: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is most likely the No 1 internal problem for the Republican party going into \u8217\'20\'1924. Independents went by somewhere around 3% for Democrats and most of them, women anyway, went because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you adopt a strident, complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I just don\u8217\'20\'19t see how you win nationally. You can\u8217\'20\'19t win Michigan. You can\u8217\'20\'19t win Pennsylvania. You do the electoral college map and I wonder how the Republicans see a road to the White House if they adopt a complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yet the likely candidates in what could be an ugly Republican primary are trying to outdo each other in attacking reproductive rights and throwing red meat to the base. Christina Reynolds, a spokesperson for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.emilyslist.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emily\u8217\'20\'19s List}}}\uc2 , which works to help elect Democratic female candidates in favour of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t just have a messaging problem; they have a policy problem.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans are already in a race to the bottom: how quickly and how drastically can we take away people\u8217\'20\'19s rights? They are misreading what the voters want. Voters have told them very clearly. That may play in parts of a Republican primary but it won\u8217\'20\'19t work for them in the end.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230121ej1l000jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
