\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not done\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  opponents hold first March for Life since fall of Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,384 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par 02:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists descend on Washington for annual march and commit to continue fighting to limit reproductive rights\par \par \uc2 Thousands of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents descended on Washington DC for the annual March for Life on Friday, the first time since achieving its foundational objective: persuading the supreme court to overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Every year since the landmark 1973 decision, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have come to the nation\u8217\'20\'19s capital to march, plead and pray for a post-Roe America where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just banned but was \u8220\'20\'1Cunthinkable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A half-century later, they gathered again on the National Mall in Washington, this time to celebrate movement\u8217\'20\'19s greatest victory. But they also came with a new mission to fighting the battles now playing out in their states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the march began as a response to Roe, we don\u8217\'20\'19t end as a response to Roe being overturned,\u8221\'20\'1D Jeanne Mancini, the president of the March for Life Education and Defense Fund, told a jubilant crowd. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy? Because we\u8217\'20\'19re not yet done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Roe fell, movement leaders have urged Republicans to use their new House majority to pass federal restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while they press for new bans and restraints at the state level. On Friday, they warned activists against complacency, with one speaker acknowledging that the decision had ushered in \u8220\'20\'1Cchallenging times of unrest and new threats to human life\u8221\'20\'1D as a reinvigorated reproductive rights movement pushes back.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not the end of our journey,\u8221\'20\'1D the Mississippi attorney general, Lynn Fitch, whose office brought the supreme court case \u8211\'20\'13 Dobbs v Mississippi \u8211\'20\'13 that overturned Roe, said from the stage before the march. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is our charge today, in this new Dobbs era, to channel that same determination and hope and prayer that has led you to these streets for 50 years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 From the White House, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  marked the occasion with a vow to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and a proclamation recognizing the 50th anniversary of the Roe v Wade decision, which falls on Sunday 22 January.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNever before has the court taken away a right so fundamental to Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in the statement. \u8220\'20\'1CIn doing so, it put the health and lives of women across this nation at risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He called on Congress to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and pledged to continue to use his limited executive authority to protect access wherever possible.\par \par \uc2 In the seven months since the supreme court ended the federal protections guaranteed by Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in America has become a patchwork of state-by-state policies. More than a dozen states have enacted sweeping bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while several more aim to take similar actions when state legislatures reconvene this year. Pending legal challenges have added to the uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Their efforts to advance new measures at the state level have been met with fierce opposition from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates. Initiatives have sprung up to help women seeking abortions travel to states where it remains legal or to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. At the ballot box, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have suffered a string of significant and unexpected defeats, including in conservative states like Kansas and Kentucky, while several Republican candidates who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans without exceptions lost high-profile races in the 2022 midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 But at Friday\u8217\'20\'19s march, the mood was undeniably joyful. Busloads of high school students, a hallmark of the event, carried signs proclaiming: \u8220\'20\'1CI am the post-Roe generation.\u8221\'20\'1D They marched alongside seasoned activists exulting in a victory that had once seemed unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe did it,\u8221\'20\'1D the Rev Lalita Smith said, recounting her \u8220\'20\'1Cexuberance\u8221\'20\'1D when the Dobbs decision was handed down. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in doing so, we brought more battles to the forefront because now every state has a right to decide what their position is going to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Activists from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal said they were working toward a more complicated goal that required changing not only the laws, but hearts and minds, too.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf anything, the pro-life movement is more important than ever before because now it\u8217\'20\'19s up to the states,\u8221\'20\'1D said Katie, a 19-year-old college student from Massachusetts who preferred not to give her last name.\par \par \uc2 The theme of this year\u8217\'20\'19s gathering was \u8220\'20\'1CNext Steps: Marching Forward into a Post-Roe America\u8221\'20\'1D, a recognition that the fight to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America has moved to Congress and all 50 state legislatures. To further underscore the shift, the march charted a slightly different course. Instead of finishing at the foot of the supreme court, they concluded at a spot located between the court building and the US Capitol.\par \par \uc2 At the rally, the speakers presented a united front, committed to the march\u8217\'20\'19s overarching vision to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the post-Roe landscape has exposed fault lines in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement as Republicans, elected officials and activists press ahead with varying demands, tactics and approaches.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat is the most ambitious we can be?\u8221\'20\'1D Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America (SBA), a leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, told reporters this week.\par \par \uc2 Dannenfelser said she would like to see Congress enact a \u8220\'20\'1Cfederal minimum standard\u8221\'20\'1D that would ban abortions after a certain point early in a pregnancy, though she was clear-eyed that the prospects of such action were dim as long as Democrats held the Senate and the White House.\par \par \uc2 The House speaker, Kevin McCarthy, welcomed the marchers to Washington, pledging: \u8220\'20\'1CYou now have a Congress that is standing up for life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Addressing the crowd, Steve Scalise, the House majority leader, touted a pair of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures that passed the chamber earlier this month, among the first actions taken by the new Republican majority. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what difference elections make,\u8221\'20\'1D he declared.\par \par \uc2 Speaking next, the Republican congressman Chris Smith of New Jersey, co-chair of the congressional pro-life caucus, said the House would soon take up a third measure that would block federal funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as the Hyde Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The gathering also drew opposition. On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group \uc2 Catholics for Choice\uc2  unfurled large banners from the rooftop of a Planned Parenthood in Washington, as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists demonstrated below. One read: \u8220\'20\'1CMost people of faith support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists disrupted a prayer service organized by the March for Life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese extremists lied, cheated and stole seats on the supreme court in order to overturn Roe,\u8221\'20\'1D Mini Timmaraju, president of Naral Pro-Choice America, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s never been clearer that they are the minority: that\u8217\'20\'19s why they had to cheat to win, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why they were defeated handily in the midterm elections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Public opinion polls since the Dobbs ruling in June have repeatedly found that a majority of Americans support access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/07/06/majority-of-public-disapproves-of-supreme-courts-decision-to-overturn-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A Pew poll conducted in July}}}\uc2  found that nearly six in 10 Americans disagreed with the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision eliminating a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while just four in 10 approved of it. Public support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has largely remained unchanged, even as the partisan divide on the issue has deepened.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday reproductive rights activists will commemorate what has now become a bitter milestone, the 50th anniversary of the Roe decision, with rallies in state capitals around the country. The events are being held under the banner: \u8220\'20\'1CBigger than Roe\u8221\'20\'1D and organizers say they hope to build on their successes in the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will mark the anniversary on Sunday with a speech in Florida, where she will rally reproductive rights supporters to fight state-level efforts to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while calling on Congress to enact federal protections.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Democrats are bracing for new attempts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a potential 2024 presidential candidate, signed into law a bill banning the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Dannenfelser, the SBA president, said Florida was a model for what is possible across the country as a new generation of emboldened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents take charge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis year, we march with fresh resolve as a brand-new pro-life movement,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re more expectant than ever that we will make new gains for women and children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230120ej1k003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
