\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  just took two big steps on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . Will they matter?\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 963 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 15:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While the decisions cannot undo \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the 13 states they exist, it could make a huge difference where the right is protected\par \par \uc2 This week, the federal government announced two decisions designed to improve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US. The first, a rule change made by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/04/abortion-pills-fda-rule-change-pharmacies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allows}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/04/abortion-pills-fda-rule-change-pharmacies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pharmacies to dispense mifepristone}}}\uc2 , one of the two drugs needed for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The second, an opinion drafted by the justice department, gives the \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  the all clear to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/olc/opinion/file/1560596/download"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continue mailing \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills}}}\uc2 , even to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is severely restricted.\par \par \uc2 How big an impact the moves will have, however, remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 These decisions cannot undo \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the 13 states where they exist. While major pharmacies such as \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS have announced they will seek certification to dispense mifepristone, a prescription for it still will not be legal in states with a ban. Anyone distributing or taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in banned states could still face severe consequences. And the justice department opinion will not protect anyone sending pills to a banned state from being prosecuted in that state, or anyone who takes the pills knowingly to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from being investigated.\par \par \uc2 But in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected, both moves could make a big difference, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 Take California as an example, which recently expanded access for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in its state constitution. Until now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills had to be dispensed by a doctor, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, or a mail order pharmacy. But even in California, many people live {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.calhealthreport.org/2022/12/21/for-many-rural-californians-abortion-isnt-accessible-heres-what-can-be-done/?utm_campaign=2023%20IHA%20Social&utm_content=233304975&utm_medium=social&utm_source=twitter&hss_channel=tw-224412041"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hundreds of miles away}}}\uc2  from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. Traveling to a clinic to pick up pills can be time-consuming, financially burdensome, and sometimes practically impossible. Even receiving pills through the mail by a doctor increases barriers for vulnerable groups.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re a young person, or someone in abusive relationships, you maybe don\u8217\'20\'19t want to receive the medications at home,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Ashley Jeanlus, an OB-GYN from California. \u8220\'20\'1CMaybe your home address is an unsafe environment. Perhaps you don\u8217\'20\'19t have time for any unnecessary delays by being sent pills in the mailing system. So this really gives individuals more power to be able to get the care that they need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ruling will see pharmacies dispensing mifepristone in states with limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that fall short of total bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a state like Florida, where there\u8217\'20\'19s a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in effect, and the legislature and governor have been considering an earlier ban or placing limits on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they might be thinking through various ways they could limit the utility and effectiveness of this policy,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , of the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey could require administration of the pills at the facility so that the provider and patient are in the same room. They could try to add additional regulations for the pharmacies that do provide this service,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Some experts believe that while the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ruling is a step in the right direction for expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in states without bans, the devil will be in the detail.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a really good thing that the government\u8217\'20\'19s weighing in but ultimately, the courts \u8230\'20\'26 might reach a different conclusion\par \par \uc2 Greer Donley\par \par \uc2 Greer Donley, associate professor of law at Pittsburgh University, points out that recent statements by major pharmacy chains such as \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS, which said they will seek certification to stock the pills, leave many questions unanswered. They don\u8217\'20\'19t, for example, clarify in which states pills will be dispensed; or whether they will be dispensed in the mail, in-person at the pharmacy, or both. Furthermore, as mifepristone is regulated differently from many over-the-counter drugs in the US, the pharmacies able to dispense the drug may be limited.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS are dispensing at every one of their locations in states that permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that\u8217\'20\'19s a really different thing than if they\u8217\'20\'19re only dispensing at a small number of locations, or if they are only dispensing via mail,\u8221\'20\'1D says Donley.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 FDA\uc2  wanted to make an even bigger impact, Donley says , they could have removed burdensome regulations around mifepristone \u8211\'20\'13 under the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s risk evaluation and mitigation strategies program \u8211\'20\'13 that don\u8217\'20\'19t exist in other countries like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://implementationscience.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13012-021-01144-w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Canada}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese regulatory tools are only supposed to be used for risky drugs. But mifepristone is an extremely safe drug. It\u8217\'20\'19s much safer than many drugs that are on the market without [the same regulation], including Viagra and Penicillin. Probably the most important thing the \uc2 FDA\uc2  could have done is it could have removed the [regulation]. Every major medical association in the United States has said that that is what the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should have done,\u8221\'20\'1D says Donley.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice opinion finds that mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, including to states with bans, does not violate the Comstock Act, a 1873 federal law that prohibits sending \u8220\'20\'1Cobscene\u8221\'20\'1D items in the mail and encompasses contraceptives and abortifacients. Donley points out that the decision is not a court option \u8211\'20\'13 and that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://msmagazine.com/2022/11/23/texas-lawsuit-abortion-pills-mifepristone-fifth-circuit-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a looming case in Texas}}}\uc2  challenging the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills could effectively undo that opinion in a few months\u8217\'20\'19 time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a few months, we could have an injunction in Texas, that states the exact opposite of the DOJ opinion,\u8221\'20\'1D says Donley. \u8220\'20\'1CSo it\u8217\'20\'19s a really good thing that the government\u8217\'20\'19s weighing in but ultimately, the courts \u8230\'20\'26 might reach a different conclusion,\u8221\'20\'1D says Donley.\par \par \uc2 But while advocates would like to see more, they have cautious praise for the administration\u8217\'20\'19s moves this week: \u8220\'20\'1CThe government is putting some oomph behind access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D says Nash.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g002el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
