\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement just had a mask-off moment in Alabama\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 16:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Alabama, pregnant women are subjected to a work around law in the name of protecting the fetus: chemical endangerment of a child\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Steve Marshall\uc2 , Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s Republican attorney general, said he sees a path to prosecuting women for having abortions in his state. This was a bit of a faux pas: a moment of letting slip the mask that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement always tries to keep on.\par \par \uc2 Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which has only limited exemptions for women\u8217\'20\'19s lives, makes providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony, punishable by up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/practices/doctors-could-face-life-prison-under-new-alabama-abortion-law#:~:text=Republican%20Gov.,a%2010%2Dyear%20prison%20term."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 99 years in prison}}}\uc2 . But like nearly all of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have sprung into effect since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ruling in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health overturned Roe v Wade last June, the law has no mechanism to prosecute women who receive abortions. But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that patients are safe from criminal charges, according to the state\u8217\'20\'19s top prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Human Life Protection Act targets \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, exempting women upon whom an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed or attempted to be performed from liability under the law,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for attorney general Marshall told Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://1819news.com/news/item/self-managed-abortions-could-still-bring-criminal-prosecution-under-child-chemical-endagerment-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1819 News}}}\uc2 , referring to the law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. However, he continued, \u8220\'20\'1CIt does not provide an across-the-board exemption from all criminal laws.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In other words, just because the Alabama \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban specifically doesn\u8217\'20\'19t allow prosecutors to go after women who have had abortions, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that those women will not be arrested and jailed for their procedures. It just means that the prosecutors will have to find another law.\par \par \uc2 Which other law? The AG pointed to one in particular: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://judicial.alabama.gov/docs/library/docs/26-15-3.2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s law}}}\uc2  barring the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cchemical endangerment of a child\u8221\'20\'1D. The law, which was originally designed to protect kids from exposure to home meth labs, makes it a felony to \u8220\'20\'1Cknowingly, recklessly, or intentionally cause or permit a child to be exposed to, to ingest or inhale, or to have contact with any controlled substance, chemical substance, or drug paraphernalia\u8221\'20\'1D. The Alabama state supreme court ruled in 2013 that the law can be applied to \u8220\'20\'1Cchildren\u8221\'20\'1D who have not been born yet \u8211\'20\'13 that is, embryos and fetuses.\par \par \uc2 What does it mean in practice? It means that legally, a pregnancy is defined as a \u8220\'20\'1Cperson\u8221\'20\'1D, and a woman\u8217\'20\'19s body becomes an \u8220\'20\'1Cenvironment\u8221\'20\'1D that she has an obligation to keep free of hazards, in deference to the wellbeing of this other \u8220\'20\'1Cperson\u8221\'20\'1D. This is how the Alabama AG is threatening to lock up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients: by defining women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies not as spheres of those women\u8217\'20\'19s own control, but as homes to house other people \u8211\'20\'13 and by defining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as hazardous \u8220\'20\'1Cdrugs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 None of this is new. The truth is that chemical endangerment laws have long been used to criminalize pregnancy, and Alabama in particular has a long, sadistic history of prosecuting women who are pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 or believed to be pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 on suspicion that they have ingested something that might endanger a fetus.\par \par \uc2 Marijuana users and those seeking opiate addiction treatment {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/sep/12/alabama-jailing-pregnant-marijuana-users-protect-fetuses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been arrested}}}\uc2  for supposedly endangering their fetuses in the state; in one county, the imprisonment of pregnant women on chemical endangerment charges or suspicion of drug use became so common that Pregnancy Justice, a national advocacy group that provides lawyers to those prosecuted for their pregnancy outcomes, had a whole {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://secure.actblue.com/donate/napwetowah"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 project dedicated to getting women out of the county jail}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The substances that women are arrested for taking need not be illegal. Sometimes, they are prescriptions. In 2021, Alabama arrested a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/jul/27/alabama-prosecuting-woman-medication-pregnant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mother of six}}}\uc2  under a felony chemical endangerment charge after she refilled her longstanding painkiller prescription while pregnant (the woman, named Kim Blalock, had an old back injury from a car accident). Charges were eventually dropped, but not before Blalock had her home raided by armed police, who scared her children and was dragged through court for months. Her pregnancy, by the way, was fine: amid all the antagonism from her state, Blalock gave birth to a healthy baby boy.\par \par \uc2 But the comments from the Alabama AG do reveal a new trend in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are being increasingly frank about their own sadism. The newest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans gaining traction in the states have no exemptions for rape or incest; some, like those of Idaho and Tennessee, have no life of the mother exemptions, or require doctors to prove the medical necessity of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fact, in court \u8211\'20\'13 provisions that incentivize medical providers to delay or deny care, to risk women\u8217\'20\'19s health and lives.\par \par \uc2 For all the efforts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to put a paternalistic spin on their work, these positions are not anomalies; they are now firmly in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mainstream. In that sense, Marshall was just being honest: hurting women has always been the point.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 13 January 2023 to reflect the fact that the name of the woman arrested for chemical endangerment is Kim Blalock. An earlier version had named a different woman.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g002at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
