\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republican-controlled House pushes for new  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,200 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2023\par 13:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bills not expected to advance in Senate but underscore Republican majority\u8217\'20\'19s legislative priorities ahead of 2024 election\par \par \uc2 The Republican-led House on Wednesday pressed ahead with a pair of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, despite warning signs that the issue had galvanized the opposition in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe v Wade last year.\par \par \uc2 Voting mostly along party lines, Republicans first approved a bill that would compel doctors to provide care for an infant who survives an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an occurrence that is exceedingly rare.\par \par \uc2 After its passage, Republicans broke into applause on the House floor as the bill\u8217\'20\'19s sponsor, congresswoman \uc2 Ann Wagner\uc2 , a Republican of Missouri, waved the text of the legislation in celebration.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, several of them wearing white in protest, remained silent. However, on the measure, two Texas Democrats broke with the party: the congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , supported it while his colleague Vicente Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez voted present.\par \par \uc2 The House also passed a non-binding resolution condemning attacks on pregnancy crisis centers, with the support of all Republicans and three Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The proposals, among the first moves made by Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 new, narrow House majority, are unlikely to be taken up by the Democratic-controlled Senate. But their passage will provide the Republican majority an opportunity to draw a sharp contrast with Democrats on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am proud that Republicans are following through on the promises that we made to the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D the majority leader, Steve Scalise, the second-highest-ranking House Republican and a staunch anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocate, told reporters this week. \u8220\'20\'1CAll life is sacred and must be protected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have long pushed so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cborn alive\u8221\'20\'1D legislation similar to the version under consideration in the House, which could carry a prison sentence of up to five years for medical workers.\par \par \uc2 Critics, including medical professionals, say such measures are based on distortions and misinformation about what is often an extremely painful and often unwanted decision to end a pregnancy. Abortions after the point of viability, which is defined as about 23 weeks, are extremely uncommon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/09/22/gop-claim-that-democrats-support-abortion-up-moment-birth/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to federal and state data}}}\uc2 . In the rare instances they do happen, they often involve serious fetal abnormalities or risks to the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, opponents say newborns are already protected by a bipartisan law passed in 2002, which established full legal rights for infants born at any stage of development.\par \par \uc2 In a floor speech, \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2 , a Democrat of New York, said the measure \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes nothing new to protect infants\u8221\'20\'1D but neither was it \u8220\'20\'1Charmless\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bill directly interferes with a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s medical judgment and dictates a medical standard of care that may not be appropriate in all circumstances, which could, in fact, put infants\u8217\'20\'19 lives at greater risk,\u8221\'20\'1D Nadler said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates also reacted to the bill. \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s be clear: doctors are already required to provide appropriate medical care by law,\u8221\'20\'1D Jacqueline Ayers, a senior vice-president at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not how medical care works. It\u8217\'20\'19s wrong, irresponsible, and dangerous to suggest otherwise.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Wagner, who has repeatedly championed the measure, argued, without evidence, that additional protections for infants born after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts were necessary because \u8220\'20\'1Cmany of these sweet little ones are denied the medical care they need to survive and thrive\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Public support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has climbed since the supreme court eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last summer. In November, voters punished Republicans for building the conservative court that overturned Roe. Despite rampant inflation and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s low approval ratings, Democrats defied expectations in November, keeping control of the Senate and limiting Republican gains in the House, where their razor-thin majority is already proving to be a challenge.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have even been met with fierce resistance in traditionally Republican states. In Montana, a conservative western state, voters rejected an initiative related to infants born after attempted abortions that is similar to the one House Republicans passed on Wednesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a top concern among conservative Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists alarmed by the backlash to the supreme court decision. Yet the early focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given some Republicans in swing districts cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe learned nothing from the midterms if this is how we\u8217\'20\'19re going to operate in the first week. Millions of women across the board were angry over overturning Roe v Wade,\u8221\'20\'1D Nancy Mace, a Republican from South Carolina, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/minutes/congress/01-10-2023/mace-breaks-on-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters on Capitol Hill}}}\uc2  on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Noting that the bills had little chance of becoming law, she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/republican-house-will-vote-bills-curtail-abortion-immigration-rcna64944"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  the move \u8220\'20\'1Ctone-deaf\u8221\'20\'1D and said Republicans were merely \u8220\'20\'1Cpaying lip-service to life\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you want to make a difference and reduce the number of abortions with a Democratic-controlled Senate, the No 1 issue we should be working on is access to birth control,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Nevertheless, Mace voted for the bills alongside her party.\par \par \uc2 Republicans also advanced a resolution, which carries no legislative weight, condemning violence against \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life facilities, groups and churches\u8221\'20\'1D which drew Democratic opposition because of its failure to address the threats targeting women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare clinics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy ignoring these acts of violence, Republicans are sending a very dangerous message that will only embolden the extremists behind them,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Diana DeGette\uc2 , a Democrat of Colorado and co-chair of the House Pro-Choice Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on the House floor ahead of the vote, DeGette urged Republicans instead to adopt a counter-resolution that would condemn acts of political violence in any form.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, arguing in favor of the resolution, said anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups had become targets of political violence since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s June decision, and denounced the department of justice\u8217\'20\'19s response to these attacks as inadequate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis resolution is very simple and its language is clear,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jim Jordan, a Republican of Ohio, who has been critical of what he says is evidence of political bias within federal law enforcement against anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups. \u8220\'20\'1CIt also calls upon the Biden administration to take action now to bring the perpetrators to justice. Who could be opposed to that?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Senate majority leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , affirmed that the bills were \u8220\'20\'1Cdoomed\u8221\'20\'1D in the Senate, where he said Democrats would act as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfirewall against this extreme anti-choice Maga Republican agenda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust months after a historically disappointing midterm election, the Maga Republican-controlled House is putting on full display their truly extreme views on women\u8217\'20\'19s health with legislation that does not have the support of the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said ahead of the House vote. \u8220\'20\'1COnce again, Republicans are proving how dangerously out of touch they are with mainstream America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights activists won another political victory this week in Virginia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at the center of a closely watched state senate race. Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/01/11/adams-concession-virginia-beach-senate-rouse/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flipped the seat}}}\uc2 , a result that will probably prevent the state legislature from enacting a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban backed by the state\u8217\'20\'19s conservative governor, Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230112ej1c001bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
