\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 She led two historic victories for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 by persuading Republicans\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,092 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2023\par 16:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rachel Sweet on the \u8216\'20\'18uphill battle\u8217\'20\'19 to protect reproductive rights in red states Kansas and Kentucky\par \par \uc2 If there were two votes that sent shockwaves through the US this year, they were in Kansas and Kentucky, and they were both about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The former, the first direct vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be brought to the public since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade, by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans in a deeply red state, was defeated by considerably more than half the electorate (59% of the vote).\par \par \uc2 The latter, in Kentucky, seemed an even harder bet: Kentucky is one of the 16 US states that, before the November vote, seemed to have more support for banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than protecting it, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/upshot/polling-abortion-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis by the New York Times}}}\uc2  from May. It also already had an outright ban in place. But the ballot initiative, also brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners, failed to pass, with 52% of voters rejecting an amendment to say there was no explicit protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 One woman was at the center of these two campaigns: Rachel Sweet. The straight-talking 31-year-old from Kansas City, Missouri, previously managed Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s public policy for the Great Plains area, before leading the campaign to defeat the Kansas initiative, and then the Kentucky one.\par \par \uc2 The way she sums up both wins is simple: if you want to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in red states, you have to target Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats are not most of the voters [in Kentucky],\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CSo you always go with a message that is the most broadly persuasive, so that you can get to your 50% plus one vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She explains that the key to winning is to understand that no two electorates are the same, and to research, poll test and work on the messages that resonate with voters in each state.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, Republicans and independents were most swayed by messages focusing on how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are an attack on personal liberty and represent government overreach.\par \par \uc2 But in Kentucky, which already has a total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has been in place since Roe fell, there was more room to focus on the reality as well as on ideology \u8211\'20\'13 and that turned out to be effective.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere were voters who were far more likely to understand the long-term ramifications of these extreme anti-choice policies, because they were already seeing how banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  impacts not only access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, but [also] treatment for miscarriages and other areas of health care in a way that is particularly concerning,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sweet.\par \par \uc2 She gives the example of a Kentuckian named Meredith, who signed up to tell her personal story for a campaign ad for Protect Kentucky Access, the group leading the No campaign, which the group ended up not airing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe was suffering a miscarriage. And her pharmacist tried to deny her prescription for the medication she needed to manage her miscarriage because it\u8217\'20\'19s part of the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen. He literally said: \u8216\'20\'18I need you to prove that you\u8217\'20\'19re actively miscarrying.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe cruelty of that situation is just really powerful,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sweet, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no need to sell people on some dystopian future. That future is already here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kentucky proved a harder race to win than Kansas, with less institutional buy-in: While campaign donations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Kansas_No_State_Constitutional_Right_to_Abortion_and_Legislative_Power_to_Regulate_Abortion_Amendment_(August_2022)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for Kansas\u8217\'20\'19s No campaign totalled $11.48m}}}\uc2 , in Kentucky, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Kentucky_Constitutional_Amendment_2,_No_Right_to_Abortion_in_Constitution_Amendment_(2022)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they reached just $6.59m}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were always ahead of our opposition. But it did feel it was an uphill battle at a lot of times,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sweet, over the phone from her apartment in Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 The Kentucky \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is still in place. But the ballot win could impact deliberations by Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court, which is considering whether to uphold the ban.\par \par \uc2 Sweet has learned to focus on meeting Republicans where they are, explaining why \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans don\u8217\'20\'19t chime with their core values \u8211\'20\'13 rather than trying to change hearts and minds on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a very complex issue that people have very complex and entrenched feelings about. People form their opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over time, for a lot of reasons, and it is not something that any campaign, no matter how message-disciplined or well-funded, can change in the span of three months,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 After the two campaigns, which saw Sweet working long days for months on end, she is taking some time to rest before she works out her next move. But it\u8217\'20\'19s clear she will have plenty of options should she want to build on her wins through another ballot initiative.\par \par \uc2 Seventeen states currently allow citizen-led referenda. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under threat in at least ten of them. Advocates in states like Ohio, Idaho and potentially Missouri have already discussed bringing such ballots in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 Sweet acknowledges the battles to come will be hard, and different in each case. In Ohio, Republicans are trying to change the threshold for citizen-led ballots to pass, from a simple majority to a 60% threshold, and Republicans in Missouri have suggested doing the same.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen red-state voters adopt or reject policies contrary to conservative politicians\u8217\'20\'19 points of view, this is always the immediate response: \u8216\'20\'18How do we restrict access to the ballot box?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D says Sweet, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to take away people\u8217\'20\'19s right to direct democracy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Of the more conservatives states that took \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions directly to voters in 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 Kentucky, Kansas and Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 none secured 60% of the vote in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 She points to the Michigan win, where advocates succeeded in enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution with 55% of the vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s huge. You don\u8217\'20\'19t usually see candidates in Michigan win with 55% of the vote. So 60% would be a very daunting obstacle to have to work around.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But she points out that the successes for the pro-choice campaign in recent months are indicative of broad, sweeping support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights across the US, regardless of geography.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw all across the country, in really progressive states, purple states and red states, that people wanted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We saw that in really tiny states like Vermont and in huge states like California,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is an issue that can win everywhere. And I\u8217\'20\'19m sure that scares the anti-choice politicians that are in office in places like Ohio.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230104ej14001fx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
