\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 More bans and creative clinics: the future of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access in a post-Roe US\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor in New York \par \uc2 1,077 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2022\par 15:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 28\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended in 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 where does that leave people seeking to terminate a pregnancy going forward?\par \par \uc2 It was a landmark year for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, for all the wrong reasons. In the summer, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , with its new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/oct/02/us-supreme-court-cases-monumental-impact-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hard-right supermajority}}}\uc2 , dismantled the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  secured almost 50 years ago through Roe v Wade. After that came a slew of laws passed in state legislatures, court battles and \u8211\'20\'13 indeed \u8211\'20\'13 some surprise victories for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates. All this has changed the way Americans can access pregnancy care and the way they think about women\u8217\'20\'19s rights. Here are the key points from 2022.\par \par \uc2 New bans abound\par \par \uc2 For some it may have felt like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights changed in the US overnight {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on 24 June 2022}}}\uc2 . But even before Roe fell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 22 states}}}\uc2  already had laws on the books that would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans likely if not inevitable once the supreme court ruled, after years of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activism.\par \par \uc2 After Roe was overturned, things became complicated. Many bans written before the June decision bounced right into effect; others were held up in court \u8211\'20\'13 and continue to be \u8211\'20\'13 and in some places, such as Michigan, local activists and politicians found ways around them.\par \par \uc2 But the reality cannot be overstated: by October, 66 clinics across 15 US states were known to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2022/10/100-days-post-roe-least-66-clinics-across-15-us-states-have-stopped-offering-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have closed}}}\uc2 , more than 80% of the total of 79 clinics open in June in those states. As of last summer, about 33 million women of child-bearing age lived in states that had or were poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2022-supreme-court-abortion-travel/?leadSource=uverify%20wall"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to}}}\uc2  Bloomberg. Some states now ban all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exception, while others allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to a certain time limit or in extreme exceptions, such as rape or incest, or to protect the life of the pregnant person. And an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/dec/13/abortion-restrictions-us-state-legislatures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 onslaught of new restrictions}}}\uc2  looms.\par \par \uc2 Legal crackdowns loom\par \par \uc2 In April 2022, 26-year-old Texan Lizelle Herrera was arrested on murder charges for allegedly self-inducing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The case was without legal basis, and was quickly dismissed, but it was a harbinger for more extreme measures since Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana tried ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-us-supreme-court-health-religion-louisiana-b73a7cfb0afc29c30d106a85c80c7c50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and failed}}}\uc2  ) to bring murder charges against people who obtained abortions. Missouri sought to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/03/19/travel-abortion-law-missouri-00018539"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban out-of-state travel}}}\uc2  for abortions. South Carolina attempted to limit even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess124_2021-2022/bills/1373.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 basic information sharing about how to access abortions}}}\uc2  online.\par \par \uc2 None of these laws passed, but they highlight the measures that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are willing to use.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proliferates\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2  made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication accessible by mail. That became a lifeline for many when many clinics closed their doors after June and organizations offering funding and travel assistance for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access went quiet while they worked out their options in the new legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 Use of these drugs has soared. One study {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/2797883?guestAccessKey=0bf9b1b5-f43a-4a80-9cd6-294fac7370cf&utm_source=For_The_Media&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=ftm_links&utm_content=tfl&utm_term=110122"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggested a threefold increase}}}\uc2  since the supreme court decision overturning Roe as part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 case out of Mississippi}}}\uc2  called Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 However, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a catch-all \u8211\'20\'13 some fear taking pills at home where an abusive partner might see, for example, or might be worried about not having a doctor physically present during an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And there are safety risks with taking the pills too far along in a pregnancy, which is hard to regulate for. But using medication where feasible is a relatively affordable, convenient and discreet option.\par \par \uc2 Clinics get creative\par \par \uc2 Roving recreational vehicles with exam rooms and labs to provide more accessible \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and boats with surgical clinics sailing on international waters, are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2022/oct/19/mobile-abortion-shuttered-clinics-on-sea-on-wheels-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just some solutions}}}\uc2  emerging since the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended.\par \par \uc2 Clinics on wheels allow doctors to meet patients on the borders of states with bans, closest to where they are traveling from. And plans for a so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  boat in the Gulf of Mexico could give much-needed new access to patients in the south.\par \par \uc2 A surprise win, and a change in political tone\par \par \uc2 If anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates thought they could further restrict the procedure by harnessing the popular vote, they probably considered deeply red Kansas a sure bet. But those conservatives received a nasty shock in that state in August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/03/kansas-abortion-vote-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 when voters roundly rejected their proposal at the ballot box}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A hearty 59% of voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/12/how-abortion-rights-won-kansas-strategies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected the ballot proposal}}}\uc2  in the referendum, which had aimed to establish that there was no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. The pro-rights victory over that measure sent a resounding message to Republicans standing in the midterms, many of whom {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/12/republicans-soften-staunch-anti-abortion-stance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subsequently toned down their positions on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , or started avoiding the topic entirely.\par \par \uc2 Doctors\u8217\'20\'19 licenses under threat\par \par \uc2 When the story emerged of a 10-year-old rape victim traveling out of state to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it was banned in her home state of Ohio, it made national news. While newspapers rushed to verify that story, however, statistics show that every year hundreds of children, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://odh.ohio.gov/wps/wcm/connect/gov/9a941c10-fc7d-40ae-8ae3-ac5757ab727f/Induced+Abortions+in+Ohio+2020.pdf?MOD=AJPERES&CONVERT_TO=url&CACHEID=ROOTWORKSPACE.Z18_M1HGGIK0N0JO00QO9DDDDM3000-9a941c10-fc7d-40ae-8ae3-ac5757ab727f-nMZbBno#:~:text=The%202020%20Ohio%20abortion%20rate,in%202019%20(Figure%204)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio}}}\uc2  and the US generally, obtain abortions.\par \par \uc2 Dr Caitlin Bernard, who provided care to the 10-year-old in Indiana, was accused of fabricating the story and breaking patient confidentiality when the story leaked. Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scribd.com/document/609219490/47-Complaint-Bernard-and-Caldwell-vs-Indiana-Attorney-General-Google-Docs#fullscreen=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opened multiple investigations into}}}\uc2  Bernard, and asked Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s medical board, which has the power to revoke a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s medical license, to punish her.\par \par \uc2 The case continues, but the implications for doctors everywhere are stark.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot\par \par \uc2 After the surprise win in Kansas, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates and pro-choice activists across the country brought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballots in the midterms, with yet more shocking results. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Voters in all five states with \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  on the ballot chose }}}\uc2  to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including in Kentucky, where campaigners hoped voters would be more anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the wins, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became explicitly protected within state constitutions in places such as Vermont, California and Michigan. And in Michigan, the referendum prevented a 1931 ban from bouncing back into action. In Montana, a bill that could have criminalized doctors for providing abortions was squarely defeated.\par \par \uc2 Those wins have been celebrated, although pro-choice activists are quick to point out that without federal legislation protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , access to the procedure will remain patchy at best, with a person\u8217\'20\'19s right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  determined more by geography than by the care they need.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221227eicr000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
