\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How Republicans are trying to block voters from having a say on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 December 2022\par 16:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ballot initiatives have proven a winning strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists \u8211\'20\'13 but Ohio Republicans want to make it harder for voters\par \par \uc2 Ohio advocates hoping to replicate a string of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights victories fear being stymied by Republican lawmakers who are attempting to make it harder to pass citizen-initiated constitutional amendments.\par \par \uc2 Ballot initiatives put directly to voters have proven a winning strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists since Roe v Wade was overturned this summer, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six referendums}}}\uc2  delivering favorable results for pro-choice advocates.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Republicans want to bring their own legislation, raising the threshold for successful ballot initiatives to 60%, up from the simple majority currently required to amend the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe instinct from conservative lawmakers to make it harder for their voters to decide on the core issues that matter to them most is fundamentally an assault on voting rights,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kelly Hall, the executive director of the Fairness Project, an organization that supports progressive ballot initiatives in red and purple states. \u8220\'20\'1CThose lawmakers know their ideological views are out of sync with their voters [and] they are trying to change the rules of the game.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not all states allow constitutional amendments, but of the 17 that do, Florida is the only state that has a 60% threshold \u8211\'20\'13 all others require a simple majority. Arizona requires a 60% threshold for amendments that affect tax policy.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio secretary of state, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , justifies the Republican effort, saying it will \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect the Ohio constitution from continued abuse by special interests and out-of-state activists\u8221\'20\'1D. It is unclear whether the Republican legislation to change the threshold has the votes to pass.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights campaigners, meanwhile, are rushing to bring the issue to voters. At present, a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is being held up in court in Ohio, and a 22-week ban is in place there in the meantime. \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/upshot/polling-abortion-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling from earlier this year}}}\uc2  suggests 52% of Ohioans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly legal, while more recent polling suggests 59.1% of registered voters in Ohio {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/news/2022/10/majority-of-ohioans-would-support-enshrining-abortion-rights-in-the-ohio-constitution-new-poll-says.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would vote to secure \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights in the state constitution}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last week, two groups announced plans to bring two initiatives asking voters whether to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution.\par \par \uc2 One campaign is being led by Protect Choice Ohio, a new group led by more than 1,000 doctors. The other was announced by Ohioans for Reproductive Freedom , a coalition that includes the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and Planned Parenthood. The two groups differ on when to bring the ballot before voters.\par \par \uc2 A 60% threshold would restrict the power of citizens to bring amendments in any arena, not just on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rachel Sweet, who ran successful campaigns challenging anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballots in Kansas and Kentucky, said she was unsurprised by the move, but conceded it would make securing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment much harder if it passes. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving to get to 60% plus one is much harder than getting 50% plus one,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers in Ohio are not alone in attempting to thwart citizen-led initiatives to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.stlpublicradio.org/government-politics-issues/2022-11-30/raising-the-bar-for-constitutional-amendments-could-stop-action-on-missouri-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri is also currently }}}\uc2  discussing an increase in the threshold for constitutional amendments, to 60% of the vote. The Fairness Project fought two similar proposals in 2022, in South Dakota and Arkansas, and successfully beat attempts to change ballot thresholds.\par \par \uc2 Following promising results for pro-choice campaigners on five \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot initiatives in California, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana and Vermont in November, the group is exploring which news states are prime for similar measures in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Not all states are safe bets. Of the 17 states that allow ballot initiatives, some \u8211\'20\'13 like California, Michigan, Massachusetts, Montana and Oregon \u8211\'20\'13 already have strong existing protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . States they are likely to focus on are ones where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently under threat that allow citizen-led ballots, such as Arizona, Arkansas, Florida, Missouri, Nebraska, Nevada, North Dakota, Oklahoma and South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Democrat {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://idahocapitalsun.com/2022/11/09/unsuccessful-idaho-lt-gov-candidate-begins-work-on-abortion-rights-initiative/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Terri Pickens Manweiler}}}\uc2 , who lost her race for lieutenant governor in Idaho , announced plans to bring an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot initiative in the state shortly after her loss. There, advocates will need signatures from 6% of the voters in at least 18 of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s 35 legislative districts to qualify for the ballot, as well as signatures from at least 6% of voters statewide.\par \par \uc2 Mike Gonidakis, the president of the Ohio chapter of Right to Life, told local press the group will organize heavily against any ballot to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio, regardless of the circumstances under which it is brought.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will lead and orchestrate the largest grassroots initiative, mark my words, in Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s history to defeat whatever it is they put on the ballot,\u8221\'20\'1D Gonidakis told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbc4i.com/news/local-news/columbus/ohio-abortion-rights-advocates-announce-dueling-ballot-initiatives/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reporters}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221219eicj0018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
